-- 公式スポットに英語の説明文（description_en）を足す（2026-10-06）
--
-- 英語で検索する人（海外の人・日本を旅行する人）にもスポットのページを読んでもらえるよう、
-- 公式スポット295件の日本語の説明文を英語にしたものを入れる。日本語の説明文に書かれた事実だけを使い、新しい事実は足していない。
-- スポットのページ（api/spot.ts）では「About this place」として日本語の下に載せ、アプリの英語表示では日本語の代わりに出す。
-- ユーザーの投稿には入れない（投稿文は翻訳しない方針）。
-- 列の追加は後方互換（古いアプリは select * でこの列を受け取っても使わないだけ）。
-- あわせて、常盤平団地 スターハウス（Rn4ZaCs9）は今も人が暮らす団地なので、海外の集合住宅と同じく住民への配慮の一文を足す
-- （2026-10-06 Shu の判断。日本語を先に直し、英語は直したあとの日本語に合わせて入れる）。
-- 日本語の説明文が訳したときのまま（md5 が一致する）で、まだ英語が入っていないスポットだけに入れる。2回流しても二重にはならない。

begin;

alter table public.spots add column if not exists description_en text;

-- Rn4ZaCs9 常盤平団地 スターハウス：住民への配慮の一文を足す
update public.spots set description = description || '今も人が暮らす住宅なので、住戸や住民にカメラを向けないよう配慮したい。', updated_at = now()
where slug = 'Rn4ZaCs9' and md5(description) = 'd07b2cf9370b5c26949d1a12e60f348a';

update public.spots as s
set description_en = v.description_en, updated_at = now()
from (values
  ('23rLw54S', 'f81d088365e078b1d4abee8e5c610073', 'An experiential artwork conceived by the artists Shusaku Arakawa and Madeline Gins, opened in 1995. Across a bowl-shaped basin of land lie tilted walls, 148 winding paths and five models of the Japanese archipelago, and your sense of balance slowly drifts out of true. Walking the slopes and maze-like paths at a quiet hour feels like wandering into the landscape of a dream.'),
  ('2USUpVzb', '3fc4e0bcf239db6a6f95208bf58c59f4', 'Liège-Guillemins is a railway station in Liège, in eastern Belgium. Designed by the Spanish architect Santiago Calatrava, it replaced the 1958 station building and opened in September 2009. A huge vaulted roof of steel, glass and white concrete, about 160 meters long and 32 meters high, covers all the platforms. What stands out is that there is no wall you could call a facade: the roof is open at both ends, and the line between inside and outside disappears. Diffused light falls through the evenly spaced white ribs of the ceiling, and even the shadows are pale and uniform, making it hard to tell the time of day or the weather. After a train leaves, the platform becomes a silent space where only the white framework goes on and on.'),
  ('2Vu5Nvv7', '40a578bf665efdb07944238597a17482', 'Terminal 4 of Madrid-Barajas Airport, opened in February 2006. It was designed by the British firm Richard Rogers Partnership (now RSHP) with the Spanish practice Estudio Lamela, and won the RIBA Stirling Prize the same year. Y-shaped steel columns support an undulating ceiling finished in bamboo, and the color of the columns shifts gradually like a rainbow from one end of the building to the other. Identical spaces lit from skylights repeat endlessly, and when few people are around, your sense of distance begins to fade.'),
  ('2nZdyNm7', 'a85dfe4e31d178f3781854ba33e69887', 'The remains of a small flower-themed amusement park in front of JR Kurahashi Station in Asahi, Chiba. Its official name was Agri Museum Sobu Hana Yuen. It opened only from spring to autumn, and a waterway wound through its long, narrow grounds so that visitors could tour the flowers by boat. It appears to have closed in the mid-2000s, and as of 2022 the entrance gate and greenhouses were still standing, half buried in plants and trees. The gate is shut and entry is prohibited, and there are homes right next door. Please just look quietly from the road in front of the station.'),
  ('2xZbLSo5', 'bb58dd8bb6bfe237f19b9cfaaa471119', 'Ikeshima once thrived as a coal-mining island, but after the mine closed its population fell sharply, and many apartment blocks now stand empty across the island. Spaces that still hold traces of everyday life, slowly being taken over by nature, give the island a distinctly liminal atmosphere. People still live on the island, so please be considerate and mind your manners when you visit.'),
  ('2yNy9E97', '593351ca03385d0f9c6da1c65f0a6dd7', 'Opened in 1952 and designed by Alexey Shchusev. White stucco Baroque-style ornament lines a yellow vaulted ceiling, from which a row of chandeliers hangs. Set into the ceiling are eight mosaics by Pavel Korin depicting generals and battles from Russian history, and the floor is red granite. A palace-like interior placed in a transfer station 37 meters underground, its function and its decoration never quite seem to match. Left alone under the lavish ceiling just after a train pulls out, you start to lose track of what this room is actually for.'),
  ('3S4gV2nM', '0e9b7137e76b1dd95b871857b1f39e23', 'The plaza of the Tsukuba Center Building, completed in 1983 at the heart of Tsukuba Science City. Designed by Arata Isozaki, it inverts Rome''s Piazza del Campidoglio: an oval plaza sunk a level below its surroundings, with a fountain at its center. Enclosed by stone steps and walls, the plaza turns very still on weekday mornings without events or on rainy nights, as if you had been left alone on a stage prepared for someone else.'),
  ('3ZXPPqYu', 'd919263d3404d27dc582f196fee9656f', 'Gateaux Kingdom Sapporo, a resort hotel that began life as the former Sapporo Terme. The corridors leading to the indoor pool and large public baths, and the area around the dining facilities, have gained a quiet reputation on social media for feeling intensely poolcore and liminal the moment the crowds thin out. The retro architecture surviving from the Terme era gives the place a nostalgic yet strangely unreal atmosphere.'),
  ('3mPAPYhW', 'a8761ea11e396b60306656634d54f3dc', 'An unfinished subway lying beneath Central Parkway in Cincinnati. Construction began in 1920, but rising costs and political conflict exhausted the funds in 1927, and work stopped before any track was laid. More than 3 km of tunnel and four underground stations, including Liberty Street, survive largely intact: there are platform edges and rows of columns, yet a train has never once arrived. A water main now runs through it, and there are no public tours. The city has been seeking proposals for its use, but entering without permission is not allowed.'),
  ('3nQekYXT', 'fce41dd3159ce0abee162bf24a148a01', 'A city hall completed in 1933 and still in use today. Its landmark is a clock tower topped with a tiled roof and shachi (mythical tiger-headed fish), and together with the neighboring Aichi Prefectural Government main building it is designated a national Important Cultural Property. The corridor on the north side runs for about 100 meters, and its view of identical windows and doors repeating into the distance has been used as a location for films and TV dramas.'),
  ('4MzTz3PL', '2b312b9aece3ab1951abc7c27cf77259', 'A tunnel through the border between Shiga and Fukui prefectures, opened in 1884 (Meiji 17) as a railway tunnel on the old Hokuriku Line and later converted for road use. It is over 1 km long, with little lighting inside, and so narrow that only one car can pass at a time, so traffic lights at both entrances control alternating one-way traffic. Walls that mix concrete with bare rock, damp air, and the small light of the exit far ahead create a closed-in feeling; long before its reputation as a haunted spot, it already has an unusual presence simply as a road tunnel. When driving through, pay close attention to oncoming traffic and the timing of the signals.'),
  ('4y7VrgGL', 'e9434fe6ae2aac28159c02caf4f46776', 'The remains of a large ryokan (traditional inn) in the middle of the Jozankei Onsen hot spring town. Founded in 1965, it closed once in 2014 and then reopened under a different company, but it has been shut since February 2020. The Jozankei Hotel right next door has also been closed since 2021, so large buildings with dark windows stand side by side in the middle of a resort town full of passing tourists. These are private buildings, so please do not enter the grounds and view them only from the streets of the town.'),
  ('55S5FZeL', '7ad027ca89d159ddd8670adcc435aa4a', 'A desert town in South Australia that produces much of the world''s gem-quality opal. Because summer days above 40 degrees are common, about half the residents live in "dugouts", homes carved out of the rock. There are also underground churches and hotels, and the rooms, with tool marks still on their walls, stay at around 23 degrees all year. Above ground, mounds of mining spoil dot the landscape and few people are about. Most dugouts are private homes, so visit those open to the public. The mining fields are full of vertical shafts, so do not enter unsigned areas.'),
  ('5QyLHMCy', 'c0ee8dcc8525311c11077f98e6a5fefe', 'Kazan-do (Kazan Tunnel) opened in 1903 (Meiji 36) as a road tunnel on the Shibutani Kaido, and has remained as a pedestrian tunnel since a new tunnel was built for National Route 1. Its brick portals are covered in moss, fallen leaves carpet the inside, and it is wrapped in dense trees. It is preserved so that pedestrians and cyclists can pass through, and in the quiet there is a feeling of standing at the entrance to another world.'),
  ('5VJb3WDb', 'f89f2c92f0aa10fb3043a6a8e3748717', 'A 105-story, 330-meter pyramid-shaped hotel on which construction began in 1987. In 1992, just as the frame reached the top, work stopped because of an economic crisis, and the building was left as bare concrete for 16 years. Construction resumed in 2008, the glass cladding was completed in 2011, and since 2018 one face of the exterior has lit up with LED displays. The interior is still unfinished and the hotel has never opened. It can be seen from almost anywhere in the city, yet only a very few people have ever been inside.'),
  ('5c4qXbrK', '9c129e2f78b7c7dafe911319b0f64859', 'Opened in 1975. Rather than smoothing the excavated bedrock, the builders left its rough surface and painted it a reddish color, turning the station into a cave-like space. The artist Sigvard Olsson imagined fragments of Kungsholmen''s history buried in soft earth and now coming to light, and set into the walls items such as chimney foundations, a 17th-century doorway and market tools. When the train leaves, only the sound of the escalators remains under the rock, and it feels less like being beneath a city than being lost inside the layers of the earth.'),
  ('5g7LAi9Y', '46fe84145a23c71c918a2d4f12b0c117', 'The Palace of Justice, home of the Ministry of Justice, completed in 1972 and designed by Oscar Niemeyer. Artificial waterfalls pour between its concrete columns into a shallow pool in front. It stands in a government district where 17 identical ministry buildings line both sides of the Eixo Monumental at equal intervals all the way to the horizon, and because the city plan was not designed for walking, hardly anyone is on foot even in the daytime. In the dry season the air is parched, shadows fall dark, and only the sound of water echoes. Sixty years on, the central axis of the "capital of the future", drawn across the grassland in 1960, still stretches out without ever having been caught up by the human scale.'),
  ('5uoQXuQ8', 'f78d9ed22dde9de5faa9c485acb46878', 'The central axis of Astana, the capital of Kazakhstan, laid out in the new city on the left bank of the Ishim River after the capital moved there in 1997. Along this axis, based on a master plan by Kisho Kurokawa, a pedestrian boulevard of a little over 2 km runs in a straight line from the presidential palace, Ak Orda, to the Khan Shatyr shopping center. Government buildings and high-rises line both sides, with gold-tinted glass and blue domes catching the eye. The buildings and paving are all new and there is little sense of daily life, so walking here feels like moving through an architect''s rendering of a finished city.'),
  ('65CEvUYF', '51f3fdc2781609fe0dfa43eb74a6c7b3', 'Boston City Hall, completed in 1968, and the roughly 3-hectare brick-paved plaza in front of it. The concrete building, whose upper floors project progressively outward, sat for many years on a vast expanse of paving with little shade (trees and a play area were added in a 2022 renovation). Though modeled on European piazzas, the plaza is too large to be filled by everyday foot traffic: wind sweeps through in winter, and in summer the glare off the bricks is strong. Only figures cutting diagonally across it and the grid of the building''s windows repeat with any regularity.'),
  ('698Asdpz', 'adf8e125c0c695de38346657f1c02e93', 'A cultural center designed by Nobuaki Furuya, opened in 2005. A walkway from the station building above the tracks at Chino Station leads straight into the complex, blurring the line between station and building. The library runs long and narrow alongside the station platform behind a 5-meter-high wall of glass. A place for waiting for trains and a place for reading sit side by side, separated by a single pane of glass: a space made for passing through.'),
  ('6JfrFZ2y', 'ccb86deeaacadf1618fd0d8a1de3b1c6', 'The site of Kyodosha Yuzawa San-ei, a locally owned department store that operated from 1982 until around 1999. The building still towers over the area in front of Yuzawa Station. Its ground floor has been hollowed out to make way for a karaoke shop and its parking lot, but the faded "SAN-EI" logo remains on the outer wall. The upper-floor windows, which still recall the store''s working days, stay quietly shut, and amid the busy street in front of the station, this one spot carries a stillness as if time had stopped.'),
  ('6sZbje49', 'bded7648a74dc7f29444ba77a6c84388', 'A 750-meter pedestrian tunnel built in 1996 to replace a gorge walking trail that had been closed after a rockfall accident in 1988. For the 2018 Echigo-Tsumari Art Triennale it was remodeled as "Tunnel of Light", a work by Ma Yansong / MAD Architects. At the end of a long passage lit by color-changing lights, at the Panorama Station, a water-covered floor mirrors the gorge and the people standing in it.'),
  ('74Dex6Z8', '785c174935b2efc5e713c2687bb336e3', 'A concert hall designed by Antonin Raymond and completed in 1961. Of its 300 million yen construction cost, 100 million was covered by citizens'' donations, and a folded-plate concrete structure, like a folding screen, roofs the large, column-free auditorium. On tour days with no performances, you can walk through the empty auditorium and lobby with the lights left off, where only the atmosphere of Showa-era public architecture quietly lingers.'),
  ('77BLLqk2', 'a91338dba77fe55e17c5bf0cbf892de9', 'The world''s first practical capsule hotel, designed by Kisho Kurokawa and opened in 1979. It is still in business today and has welcomed guests across three eras: Showa, Heisei and Reiwa. The corridors lined with stacked capsules form an unreal, uniform space where identical private pods continue on and on, and the late-night lobby and dim passages between the capsules carry a peculiar floating feeling, cut off from everyday life. An architecturally important liminal space that is still in active use.'),
  ('79p4o7ue', 'c413d1c933aea11696a2318ca64e7c79', 'A concrete track about 1,300 meters long with 13 curves, built on the slopes of Mount Trebević for the 1984 Sarajevo Winter Olympics. It continued to host World Cup events after the Games, but during the conflict of the 1990s it was used as an artillery position, and it was later abandoned. Today the U-shaped chute snakes down through the forest, its walls covered in graffiti. Some areas of the surrounding forest may still carry a risk of landmines, so do not leave the track and the maintained paths.'),
  ('7XgJ2pw4', '889c94087e44d927fd8a6634f440fdb1', 'After independence in 1991, the center of the capital was rebuilt with one white marble-clad building after another, and in 2013 the city was recognized by Guinness World Records as having the highest density of white marble buildings (543 of them). Government offices and apartment blocks alike are finished in the same white, with wide avenues, golden domes and squares lined with statues, yet few people are seen walking. The overly tidy streets can look like a life-size model. Photographing government buildings and the presidential palace is prohibited, and police may stop you, so be careful.'),
  ('7eD74Vfb', 'a66d1be40a95844f2ab0b93489287a24', 'A shopping complex in Asakita Ward, Hiroshima, opened in 1991 with the Fresta supermarket as its anchor tenant. The interior keeps much of the look of the early Heisei years, and there is a nostalgic air inside, as if time had stopped. A liminal space with a dreamcore, Heisei-retro feel, where nostalgia and a sense of unreality sit side by side.'),
  ('7gRLUSMJ', '38abe1ae6d4debcd2698bcc133fcf6e9', 'A railway bridge of the former Takachiho Railway spanning the deep valley of the Gokase River in Takachiho, Miyazaki. Standing about 105 meters above the riverbed, it was known as the tallest railway bridge in Japan. Service was suspended after Typhoon No. 14 in September 2005, and the line was abandoned in 2008 without being restored. Today Takachiho Amaterasu Railway runs a sightseeing vehicle, the "Grand Super Cart", and you can look down into the valley through its glass floor. On windy days it may turn back without crossing the bridge.'),
  ('84YSwrm3', 'c652dc169f007d5178330b78d4ac5dbb', 'A cultural center designed by Zaha Hadid and completed in 2012. The outer skin, floors and ceilings are joined as one continuous curved surface, and there is no boundary anywhere between wall and floor. Inside the pure white interior there are no straight lines or corners to help you judge distance, and light wraps smoothly around the surfaces, so the depth of the space never settles. The handrails curve, the stairs curve, and even in the auditorium bands of wood ripple around the seats. Sound does not bounce off corners either, so footsteps fade away with a strange softness. Having designed away the very idea of boundaries, it is a building where you cannot tell whether you are inside it or simply passing through.'),
  ('8QNryKdW', 'c18caf314a5e2f79667dae9068822b59', 'The eastern terminus of the Stockholm metro''s Blue Line, opened in 1977. The platform lies about 34 meters underground inside the bedrock, and the artist Ulrik Samuelson designed the station''s decoration. Between rock walls painted green and red stand statues and ornamental fragments from buildings demolished during the redevelopment of the city center, giving it the feel of a garden sunk into the earth. Work is underway on a new line extending south from here, and the station was temporarily closed in summer 2026.'),
  ('8dDucTfE', '979a8bbabb749e070b2e178bbd7e1e66', 'A railway tunnel on the Chuo Main Line, opened in 1903. It fell out of use in 1997 when a new tunnel was built alongside it, and today it is a walking path about 1.4 km long that you can walk for free. The brick walls, soot from steam locomotives, rails and signs remain as they were, and you walk along a perfectly straight passage with lights spaced at regular intervals, about 30 minutes each way.'),
  ('8hv3niCX', '4b7b892e030a799591f1ab12cd69ff55', 'The terminus of the former JNR Taisha Line, which was closed in 1990. The wooden Japanese-style station building, built in 1924 with a design evoking Izumo Taisha shrine, is a national Important Cultural Property, and the platform and a D51 steam locomotive also remain. It reopened to the public in April 2026 after conservation work. Standing on a platform where trains will never come again, you feel as if time stopped partway through the timetable.'),
  ('8pmZvV4M', '740182e1358737a98a51912cb868876d', 'The terminus of the Wakasa Railway, opened in 1930 and the easternmost station in the Chugoku region. Its wooden station building is a Registered Tangible Cultural Property. Beyond the tracks remain a hand-operated turntable, a water tower, a coaling stage and a C12 steam locomotive: equipment from the age of steam, lined up and no longer in use.'),
  ('8uyxRJZG', '583b38e682716cd9080a21c9394ae56e', 'T-Centralen is the central station of the Stockholm metro and the only one where all three lines, Green, Red and Blue, meet. The Green Line platforms opened in November 1957, and the deepest, the Blue Line platforms, were added in August 1975. On the Blue Line platforms the excavated bedrock is left as the ceiling and walls, covered with blue-and-white vine patterns by the artist Per Olof Ultvedt. The plant motifs, painted to follow the uneven surface of the cave, and the blue lighting give this deep rock space an underwater color. It is a busy interchange, yet when the crowds ebb just before the last train or early in the morning, the blue cave is left behind with only its painted vines.'),
  ('9Gg5tgdq', 'c6cffed330691974827b78e49cb69f54', 'An abandoned hotel on the seafront along National Route 135 near Izu-Okawa Station in Higashiizu. It is said to have opened in the early 1960s and appears to have closed around 2004. The main building facing the sea still stands; most of its windows are gone, and it is deteriorating in the salt wind. The ownership of the land and building is reportedly complicated, which is said to be why demolition has not progressed. The building is clearly visible from the highway, so please do not enter the grounds, watch out for traffic, and view it only from outside.'),
  ('9UgkK4kQ', '6b6ea3fe8f050455a6059622817990e3', 'The remains of a strip theater in the hot spring town of Oigami Onsen in Numata. It appears to have closed by around 2000. It is an unusual building: tallest at the front facing the road, with the roof growing lower and the building narrower toward the back. At the reception, lettering showing a "2500" admission price and a 7 p.m. curtain time still remains, recalling the days when the town thrived as a hot spring resort for nightlife. It is a private building surrounded by inns and homes that are still in use, so please just look quietly from the street.'),
  ('9aLUxoT8', 'ef6ea4ab9eaad225a3382df75bb1fea5', 'A nitrate-works town founded in the late 19th century that supported Chile''s saltpeter industry. It had a school, a theater, a hotel, a market and even a swimming pool said to have been built from ships'' steel plates, and around 1940 some 3,700 people lived here. As synthetic fertilizers spread, demand for saltpeter collapsed, and the works closed in 1960. The dryness of a desert where it almost never rains has preserved the buildings, and the theater''s wooden seats and the pool''s steps are still there. It became a World Heritage Site in 2005 and is now open to the public as a museum.'),
  ('9dLFK2qh', '2b07166736ec1d52e1298060b0769580', 'Opened in 1996 and designed by Oscar Niemeyer. On a rocky point jutting into Guanabara Bay, a saucer-like gallery 50 meters across sits on a cylindrical base, surrounded at its foot by a reflecting pool. A red ramp sweeps up from the ground in a wide curve to the entrance, and as you climb, the bay and the far shore slowly turn around you. Inside, a band of windows wraps around the central gallery, looking out over Rio de Janeiro and Sugarloaf Mountain. The slope you walk before entering the building is itself a passage that draws out the moment of arrival.'),
  ('A74rJrvL', '0987e45e19579bd37a38f660dfc3f005', 'A public housing estate in Shek Kip Mei. Residents began moving in in 1977, and by 1979 all eight blocks were complete. Blocks painted pale green and blue enclose a plaza on three sides, and old playground equipment remains on the podium above the market. The view of identical windows and corridors became known through photographs, and more and more people now come to take pictures. About 6,000 people still live here, so please stay quiet when you visit and take care not to point your camera at homes or residents.'),
  ('AQ8Q9NVr', 'bda269e94dcc5a66a330b3f20af970b3', 'A railway museum housed in a 1999 reconstruction of the old Tsuruga Port Station building. The building, a faithful reproduction of Taisho-era Western-style architecture, is a valuable link to the history of Japan''s railways. Its retro exterior standing in the quiet of Kanegasaki Ryokuchi park, with few people around, creates a strange sense that time has stopped. When it is lit up at night it takes on an even more dreamlike air, and it has drawn attention as a liminal space drifting on the border between the present and the past.'),
  ('AjQzCCkp', 'c67b2d41dd9b8c1e09c287f409861069', 'The site of a station on the former JNR Shihoro Line, once busy with the forestry industry. Trains had stopped calling here nine years before the line was abolished, and by the time it closed the area was already almost uninhabited. The platform and tracks are still preserved, and the line, slowly disappearing into the grass, runs straight into the depths of the forest with a stillness as if only time had been left behind. In a place where human activity ended long ago, the rails alone remain, standing out strangely in the deep forest.'),
  ('AreEbsxm', '5012f2d55cefc742c6371124cf938037', 'A free observation deck on the 31st floor of the Bandaijima Building at Toki Messe, facing Niigata Port, about 125 meters above the ground. The building was completed in 2003. From the quiet, glass-enclosed floor, at night you can see only the city lights and the dark Sea of Japan. With few people around after dark, the observation deck falls silent like a waiting room for a journey that goes nowhere.'),
  ('BEkiN7yc', 'b2348dd2aac5a74ae38bccb4c18b84c1', 'The southernmost covered shopping arcade in Japan, at the heart of Ishigaki Island. Two arcades were built over the street in 1988 and 1989, with the public market sitting between them. By day it is busy with tourists, but in the morning before the shops open, or after they close, all that remains is a long, dim passage where the roof keeps out the strong southern sun.'),
  ('BQQBELWM', '536c04ee015870e4d0b4285e8bffe33f', 'A concrete arch bridge of the former JNR Shihoro Line, standing in Lake Nukabira in Kamishihoro, Hokkaido. Completed in 1937, it is 130 m long with eleven arches, and it went out of use in 1955 when the line was rerouted for the construction of Nukabira Dam. As the reservoir level rises and falls, the bridge emerges above the water from winter to spring and sinks beneath it from summer to autumn. It crumbles a little more each year, and part of a side wall collapsed again in April 2026. To see it up close, either borrow the key to the forest road gate (reservation required) or join a guided tour. It can also be seen in the distance from an observation deck along the national highway. Please do not climb onto the bridge.'),
  ('BYxVgBv8', 'ca2eca731f3a6446744f27874b9bb8a7', 'Sri Lanka''s second international airport, opened in 2013 near Hambantota in the south of the country. Most of the construction cost was financed by loans from the Export-Import Bank of China, and it was expected to handle one million passengers a year, but scheduled flights never took hold and it was reported as "the world''s emptiest international airport." The terminal, complete with boarding bridges and a departure lounge, has almost no sign of people during the hours without flights. The surrounding area is also elephant habitat. In recent years seasonal charter flights have operated, and handing its operation to a private company has been under consideration.'),
  ('BZHyPP3o', '74de4ddc511a12b2fab15afbca2ebd2e', 'An underground walkway at Chicago O''Hare''s Terminal 1, linking United Airlines'' Concourses B and C. It was completed in 1987 along with the terminal designed by Helmut Jahn, and along its ceiling runs Michael Hayden''s neon artwork "The Sky''s the Limit," about 227 m long. Its 466 neon tubes flow and shift color while an arrangement of "Rhapsody in Blue" plays softly. It is located inside the security checkpoint, so only passengers can walk through it.'),
  ('C3r5rXkW', 'ebcdf54f74d7c8c7ccd1a8efa817f353', 'The remains of a mining headquarters spread across the mountainside of the Besshi Copper Mine, sometimes called the "Machu Picchu of the East." The mining headquarters was located here from the Taisho era into the early Showa era, and the area thrived as a mountain town until 1968. It was then left deserted in the mountains, and red-brick and stone structures such as ore storage bins and an incline are layered across the steep slopes. On mornings wrapped in thick fog, the scene can feel dreamlike, recalling an ancient ruin in South America.'),
  ('CMzSqtkq', 'ec5710981bf3e0e65e6152eb453cb9c9', 'The remains of a small auto repair shop along Prefectural Route 5 in Inashiki, Ibaraki. It appears to have opened around 1970 and seems to have closed early on. The steel-framed workshop and the two-story building are covered in ivy, and only the shop name, written right to left on the outer wall, tells of the workplace that once was. It is private property, so please view it only from outside.'),
  ('Cc7VvnrT', 'dd7035c677ce352a6f32acd182e05222', 'A metro station in Solna, just outside Stockholm, opened in August 1975 with the Blue Line. The ceiling of the platform, carved from bare bedrock, is painted entirely red, and a green spruce forest is painted along the walls for about a kilometer, so that a forest at sunset surrounds the station. The artwork is by Anders Åberg and Karl-Olov Björk and takes as its theme the environmental destruction and rural depopulation debated in 1970s Sweden. When the train leaves, quiet returns to the red rock cave.'),
  ('CciNUdTX', 'dac7e942a3aae2850921c6ff81d2e1c6', 'A game corner with a lingering Showa-era feel, attached to a boat rental at Lake Sagami. It is run by a long-established boat business that has operated since around the time Sagami Dam was built, and vintage machines, such as a 1970s whack-a-mole game that costs from 10 yen, still work. In this space where only electronic sounds echo through the stillness of the lakeside, there is a nostalgia that feels slightly cut off from everyday life.'),
  ('Cfe4zrW6', '775e9ba972f51365297e2d60efe38237', 'The remains of the Temiya Line, part of the first railway in Hokkaido, which opened in 1880; the line was closed in 1985. The tracks were left in place, and about 1.6 km of them in central Otaru have been turned into a walking path. Rails and level crossings where trains no longer run stretch straight on between the buildings.'),
  ('D4J7oXio', 'a3e86f6a31af5613c55cd474bd4a880d', 'A Bank of Japan branch built in 1912 in Ironai, Otaru, an area once lined with banks and known as the "Wall Street of the North." It was designed by Nagano Uheiji and Okada Shinichiro under the guidance of Tatsuno Kingo. After the branch closed in 2002, it opened to the public free of charge as a museum of currency and finance. The former banking hall with its high open ceiling and the massive vault door remain inside, and the building holds the quiet of a bank whose work is done.'),
  ('Db7cLF5y', 'cdf349a23f8e190ec9b3f15b96a620fe', 'A rail, metro and bus interchange in northeastern Lisbon, opened in May 1998 for Expo ''98, the Lisbon World Exposition held that year. It was designed by Santiago Calatrava. The roof over the elevated platforms is supported by white steel columns that branch out at the top to hold up the glass, and it has been likened to a grove of palm trees or the vaulted ceiling of a Gothic cathedral. Light falling between the branches casts a net of shadows on the platforms, and in the gaps between trains only the white framework and its shadows remain. It is also a place where everyone is on their way somewhere else.'),
  ('Dio8uQN2', '55e0e4aa482ecf49af7f087ae62a984d', 'A high-speed rail station designed by Zaha Hadid, opened in 2017. The station building itself is a white bridge that curves across the tracks for more than 400 m, and inside is a curved concourse lined with bone-like ribs. It lies about 15 km northeast of central Naples, surrounded by fields and roads. Ridership has been lower than planned, and the vast concourse is often nearly empty. With no straight lines to orient by, you lose track of how far you have walked.'),
  ('DmNxYTtA', '4210c9c999b64384346e9a1484a0617b', 'The central district of Yubari, a city that once prospered from coal mining and was called the "coal capital." After the city''s financial collapse in 2007, its population fell sharply. Signs and buildings from the old shopping streets remain as they were, while many have become vacant or abandoned. In streets that seem frozen in the early 2000s there is hardly any sign of people, and shuttered shops alternate with vacant lots overgrown with weeds. The rapid shrinking of a regional city is laid bare here, with a particular emptiness that seems to reflect Japan''s future.'),
  ('DpsXBFr5', '8dd7a83171787baf118a77191aa7d949', 'The remains of an amusement park on the shore of Lake Kejonuma in Osaki, Miyagi. It opened in 1979 as Kejonuma Hoyo Land, with a Ferris wheel, a merry-go-round, go-karts and a hotel with a radon hot spring, and closed in 2001. The Ferris wheel still stands in the overgrown grass, and the site is sometimes used as a filming location for movies and TV dramas. The grounds are private property and entry is prohibited, so please view it only from the city road along the lake.'),
  ('DvnXSY6X', '1f7ef55be06a193763f3b951af1e9bff', 'A biological research institute in La Jolla, California, designed by Louis Kahn at the request of Jonas Salk and completed in 1965. Between the facing laboratory buildings lies a travertine plaza, which, on the advice of the architect Luis Barragán, was left without plants, with only a single narrow channel of water running down its center. Beyond the channel the Pacific horizon opens up, and around the spring and autumn equinoxes the sun sets in line with it. As a working research facility, the courtyard can only be visited on architecture tours booked in advance.'),
  ('EQ4XUbmu', '41d3c5b55f4e9b2adabbd0bb620b794e', 'A hangar built in 1999 and 2000 by the company CargoLifter to construct the CL160, a large cargo airship. It is 360 m long, 210 m wide and 107 m high, and is said to be among the largest halls in the world without interior columns. After the company went bankrupt, it opened in 2004 as an indoor tropical resort. A sandy beach, a lagoon and a rainforest of around 50,000 plants are all contained beneath the steel framework, and some of the membrane is transparent so the sky shows through. The interior is kept at 26°C all year, and the seasons and weather outside never reach it.'),
  ('ETbKd2jH', '87ae0673833f23e2065c496f198b80d8', 'An unfinished holiday home development near Mudurnu in Bolu Province, northwestern Turkey. Construction began in 2014, but the developer went bankrupt in 2018, leaving 587 half-built houses of a planned 732 on the hillside. Identical little castles with spires and conical roofs stand at regular intervals, and the same scene repeats from whichever street you stand in. Only the shape of a town where no one has ever lived remains. The site is fenced and guarded, so please do not enter without permission and view it from outside.'),
  ('EeKfeyAg', 'd50123dcf3fd6ecf66e843afad0cdd7b', 'The Gorakukan, a recreation hall for the miners and their families at the Mitsui Bibai coal mine, which once boasted the largest coal output in the East. It also served as a cinema and in its heyday was a lively gathering place for the mining community. More than half a century after the mine closed, its concrete walls have been almost completely swallowed by ivy and trees, and the interior seen through the openings sinks into darkness. Buried in green, it quietly holds the memory of a mining town once crowded with people.'),
  ('Ej6x5s75', '685a0d6d3e97c57621f309e7f81d9409', 'An observation and exhibition room on the 14th floor of the Port Building, a 100 m tower built in 1999 to mark the 100th anniversary of the opening of the Port of Yokkaichi. Outside the windows, about 90 m above the ground, the port and the petrochemical complex spread out, and at night only the lights of the factories flicker quietly. On weekdays the observation room has few visitors, becoming an empty room that looks down on factories that never stop working.'),
  ('EozrwoNy', '3e8eedc1c8038b6ae4ce4a9cb1d44832', 'A transfer station on the Kaohsiung metro in Taiwan where the Red Line and Orange Line meet, opened in 2008. The ceiling of its circular concourse is covered by "The Dome of Light," a stained-glass work by the Italian-born glass artist Narcissus Quagliata. About 30 m in diameter and made of more than 4,500 pieces of glass, it follows four themes, Water, Earth, Light and Fire, tracing a cycle from the birth of life to destruction and rebirth. When the flow of people pauses, it feels like standing at the bottom of a giant underground kaleidoscope.'),
  ('ErGwgdUJ', '0f87c5b07200d9eb52001e6dfc65f079', 'An underground shopping street that runs straight for about 400 m above the Namboku subway line, from Odori to Susukino. It opened in 1971, the year before the Sapporo Olympics. The passage is packed with people during the day, but when the shop shutters are down or the whole mall is closed, it becomes just a long underground corridor of near-identical storefronts, where the view never changes however far you walk.'),
  ('EuDSmVMc', 'd8902385a0e42318f066e163eddb4379', 'A long, narrow underground shopping street running 240 m in a straight line east from Fushimi subway station. It was created in 1957 as an underground wholesale arcade linking the station with the Chojamachi textile district above. Shops line only one side of the passage, and the walls painted yellow-green and orange, along with the exposed pipes, seem to preserve the atmosphere of its early days. Before the shops open or after they close, the passage becomes a long corridor where the same view goes on and on.'),
  ('F8ZXZ5kV', 'dfe0ca9a0b14973fc361160693843047', 'A 31-story council housing block in west London, designed by Ernő Goldfinger and completed in 1972. A slender elevator tower stands apart from the residential block, connected to it only by bridges every three floors. Since the elevators stop only at those floors, the corridors feel neither inside nor outside the housing. After a period of decline following its completion, management improved thanks to residents'' efforts, and in 1998 it became a Grade II* listed building. People still live here, so please view it only from outside.'),
  ('FGg4w6Jh', '635a5fcb54a9034de9a26b015ef123a5', 'Opened in 1978 as Narita Airport Station along with the opening of the airport, it was renamed Higashi-Narita Station in 1991 when a new station was built directly beneath the airport terminal. It is still the terminus of the Keisei Higashi-Narita Line, with through services to the Shibayama Railway, but its former limited express platforms are no longer used, and station signs bearing the old name and old advertisements remain. Outside the ticket gates, an underground passage about 500 m long leads to Terminal 2, and at quiet times only footsteps echo through it. Though still a working station, it has a stillness as if time had stopped.'),
  ('Ff8i4vwk', 'd9273168efad2294bbf835ef38ac6bbc', 'A large Ferris wheel on the roof of the Iyotetsu Takashimaya department store in Matsuyama. Built into the building itself, it began operating in 2001 when the store was expanded, rising 85 m above the ground with a diameter of 45 m. Its 32 four-person gondolas take about 15 minutes to make one rotation, offering views over the city of Matsuyama. Two transparent see-through gondolas were added in 2016. Above the sales floors where shoppers come and go, the gondolas keep turning slowly, with a quiet that feels slightly lifted from the city.'),
  ('G5VgyPMb', 'f8d761bd7d3d1b404566e2fca99eb692', 'The ruins of the smelter and ore dressing plant of the Osarizawa Mine, once said to be the finest in the East. Moss-covered concrete colonnades are wrapped in mountain mist, and below them settling ponds filled with emerald-colored water spread out quietly. The ruins, which bear the memory of industry, are slowly returning to the green and water of nature, creating a dreamlike space.'),
  ('GE9H3gyW', 'ca3c8dfc3b3800f626b03b8e5adcf531', 'A submerged forest that appears only in spring on the reservoir of Shirakawa Dam in Iide, Yamagata. A grove of white willows that normally stands on dry land is flooded partway up the trunks from late March to mid-May, when snowmelt fills the reservoir. On windless mornings the trees are mirrored on the surface, and the line between water and sky disappears. When the water level is lowered to prepare for summer floods, the trees return to dry land. Canoe tours that paddle through the forest are also available. Park in the lakeside park''s parking lot, not on the road.'),
  ('GGaTzjiy', '32ca1c416b298c97383a279510fc4012', 'A station on Naples Metro Line 1, opened in 2012 and designed by the Spanish architect Óscar Tusquets. A cone-shaped shaft, the "Crater de Luz" (crater of light), runs from street level deep underground, and an LED work by Robert Wilson lights the blue mosaic lining it in constantly shifting colors. Riding the long escalators down through the blue feels like sinking to the bottom of the sea. When the crowds thin out, there is a stillness like being left alone in an artificial deep sea.'),
  ('GL4Ht6wV', 'dbca5b3d01af5cb07281f46e9325227d', 'Pieri Moriyama, a large shopping mall on the shore of Lake Biwa. It opened with many tenants, but they pulled out one after another, and for a time nearly every shop had its shutters down, drawing nationwide attention as a "bright ruin." Since a full renewal in 2014 it has become lively again, yet its wide corridors and atriums still hold a distinctive atmosphere in which the memory of its former bustle sits alongside a certain quiet.'),
  ('GLu6uDS2', 'a1f7817cd9679cc369a90508eefec9ec', 'A coal-mining town founded by Sweden in 1910 and bought by the Soviet Union in 1927. It had a palace of culture, a gymnasium, a swimming pool, greenhouses, and what is said to be the world''s northernmost statue of Lenin, and in the 1980s about a thousand people lived here. Mining ended in March 1998, and by October of the same year no permanent residents remained. The cold, dry climate has slowed the decay of the buildings, and much of the equipment is still in place. A hotel now operates here, and a small staff stays during the summer. Polar bears roam the area, so if you visit, go with a tour.'),
  ('GSVYrtzL', '5da199af8591d9e34e67a69f1e18deee', 'A library opened in 2011, designed by Coelacanth K&H (Hiroshi Horiba and Kazumi Kudo). About 6,000 round windows are set into its white, box-like walls, and points of light fall quietly into a large, high-ceilinged space. Set under the wide suburban sky, the white box looks, even from outside, like a building slightly removed from reality.'),
  ('GUck25bU', '37d37775839779c2aa9b880949532a92', 'An observation lobby on the 21st floor, the top floor of the Shizuoka Prefectural Government Annex, completed in 1996. Anyone can go up for free without a reservation or checking in, and on clear days you can see Mt. Fuji and Suruga Bay. At quiet times, such as weekday evenings, there is only the plain, unadorned interior of a government building and the view filling the windows, spread out in an odd stillness.'),
  ('GXN92tps', '7ae89808b53f799cd9d3790f6f91cae9', 'A former shopping street in central Miyazaki City, said to have grown out of a postwar black market. Step off the busy Tachibana-dori arcade into a side street and you find a scene where time seems to have stopped. The corrugated iron roofs are rusted and crumbling, and shuttered shops still bearing old signs such as "Onitsuka Shoten" line the lane, while a few shops scattered among them are still in business, giving it a strange atmosphere where ruin and everyday life exist side by side.'),
  ('GeYmXhaQ', 'a15fe9bdc5a239206d9187627cb5b322', 'Nago City Hall, completed in 1981 and designed by Team Zoo (Atelier Zo). It was chosen from 308 entries in a nationwide design competition and won the Architectural Institute of Japan Prize. Lattices of concrete block and sunshade trellises are stacked in tiers, and breezy semi-outdoor spaces open up throughout the building. On holidays or in the evening, when no one is around and only the wind passes over the stepped terraces and long ramps, it looks like a strange place that is neither quite a government office nor quite a village.'),
  ('GhMXEj8q', '36e6ddc624b757b000efe4f3af7af98a', 'A round, upscale panoramic restaurant that opened in 1968 on a hilltop in Monsanto Forest Park. Designed by the architect Chaves da Costa, it offered views of Lisbon and the river through its glass walls. It was later used as a disco, a bingo hall, and a warehouse before closing in 2001. It reopened as a viewpoint in 2017 but was closed again in 2023 for safety reasons. The interior walls are covered in graffiti. Security guards now patrol the site and you cannot go inside, so please view it only from outside.'),
  ('Gi6qijiq', '01bf58e6e1b119c42c0f7b2cfe6f97a9', 'A hexagonal, three-story wooden hall 16.5 m tall, built in 1796. Inside, a double-helix ramp keeps the way up separate from the way down, and because it is one-way, you never pass other visitors. After spiraling up and back down, you find yourself leaving from a different spot than where you entered. A building with a curious route, made more than 200 years ago.'),
  ('H3TU5VjM', 'd85d6acd4a8d84c0914aad82d2dc24d8', 'A white, box-shaped building in EUR, a newer district in southern Rome, also known as the "Square Colosseum." It was built from the late 1930s for the Rome World''s Fair planned for 1942, but the fair was never held because of the war. Each face has 54 arches, nine across and six high, and behind every arch lies a shadow of the same depth. Statues line the ground floor. It is often compared to a painting by de Chirico. Since 2015 it has housed Fendi''s headquarters, and only the ground floor is open to the public, during exhibitions.'),
  ('H4waUzkv', 'cf3093545b601c168113fc27b2779385', 'A mixed-use shopping complex in central Okazaki that was once busy with a supermarket, restaurants, and other tenants. As tenants have continued to leave, many of its floors and corridors now show almost no sign of people. Its interior and signage, which keep the feel of the Showa and Heisei eras, have drawn attention on social media for their striking "abandoned" atmosphere. It is a liminal space with a strange feeling of being cut off from reality.'),
  ('HDnTRVqb', '25a0d14dd396fdd3bc3a7bca84133e6a', 'A red-brick building designed by Kingo Tatsuno and Manji Kasai, built in 1911 as the head office of Morioka Bank. It is a nationally designated Important Cultural Property, was used as a bank until 2012, and has been open to the public since 2016. In the former banking hall with its high ceiling, only the counter remains, with no customers or clerks, as if time stopped for a bank that has finished its work.'),
  ('HLy7yJkZ', '050baf788376588cf380d520d85249e6', 'Spring Town, a southern European-style indoor spa where you bathe in swimsuits, part of the Spring Park that opened in 1990 at Spa Resort Hawaiians in Iwaki. It is usually crowded, but it became a talking point when the resort''s official X account shared photos taken when no one was around, introducing it as "dreamcore" and "said to be a liminal space." Other guests are present during opening hours, so follow the facility''s rules when taking photos and take care not to include other visitors.'),
  ('HTpfFcWy', '20af266804c0a3c348fab8e5ed935e89', 'A complex of cultural facilities in Valencia, Spain. Construction began in 1996 on the old bed of the Turia River, which was diverted after the great flood of 1957, and the buildings opened one after another between 1998 and 2009. Most were designed by Santiago Calatrava, while the aquarium was the work of Félix Candela. An eye-shaped planetarium, a skeleton-like science museum, and an arched promenade stand in a row along the long, narrow site. The spaces between the buildings are wide, with little but shallow reflecting pools and white paving.'),
  ('HahW24gp', 'c99f2683cde123d859b9a3b5df202e4a', 'An unstaffed station on the Echigo TOKImeki Railway''s Nihonkai Hisui Line. In 1969, with the double-tracking of the Hokuriku Main Line, it was moved inside the Kubiki Tunnel, which is about 11 km long. The platforms lie about 40 m below the station building at ground level, and from the ticket gate it is 290 steps down to the platform for trains in one direction and 280 steps to the platform for the other. When trains pass, strong winds blow through the tunnel, so the platforms are separated from the passageways by heavy sliding doors.'),
  ('HhBNjW66', '21d0f2912bd4e5f20f9697af943e1469', 'A council housing estate in Camden, north London, designed by Neave Brown and built from 1972 to 1978. An eight-story block facing the railway shields the estate from train noise, and inside it Rowley Way, a car-free pedestrian street, curves gently along. Stepped terraced homes line both sides, with similar railings and openings repeating into the distance. In 1993 it became the first postwar council housing to be listed at Grade II*. People still live in its roughly 520 homes, so please be considerate of the residents and their homes and keep quiet when taking photos.'),
  ('HmgSvMMV', 'ebc8e78c39c2d6f7336f14be55766749', 'The former village office of Okawa, Kochi, which lies at the bottom of the Sameura Dam reservoir. The village, which opposed the dam, is said to have built a reinforced concrete office in Funato, its central district that was slated to be flooded. When the dam was completed in the 1970s, the building sank into the lake along with the village center and its main settlements. It is normally hidden, but it emerges when drought sharply lowers the reservoir, and during the summer 2026 drought it reappeared down to its first-floor entrance for the first time in more than a decade. The lakebed is off limits, so please view it from the lakeside road or similar spots. Please also keep in mind that for former residents, this is part of their home.'),
  ('J7mcK3Ti', '994af49d1168ee1cc5986bfbbad5fcac', 'A building constructed as the Japanese government''s pavilion for the 1975 International Ocean Exposition in Okinawa. It stands in a corner of the large park created on the former expo grounds and is still open to the public. Much of the exhibition dates from the expo itself, and walking among the large canoes and half-century-old displays quietly arranged inside feels like being in the continuation of a festival that has already ended.'),
  ('J9ANFBvC', '1e28f5877ecb45b35ac4336ea4df4705', 'A terminal station built in 1904 as the showpiece of New York''s first subway. Guastavino tile vaults curve one after another, brass chandeliers hang down, and glass skylights are set into the ceiling arches. But the platform''s tight curve made the gap between train and platform dangerous once cars grew longer, and the station closed to passengers in 1945. Since then the tracks have continued to serve as the turnaround loop for the 6 train, so trains pass through every day, yet no one gets off. The headlights of trains that never stop light up the decorative tiles for a moment, and then it goes dark again. A station that trains have passed through for eighty years without ever arriving.'),
  ('JMeEx2vN', 'f5d387e79d1026b19b75dcb6955f5962', 'A large housing estate in southwestern Vienna where about 9,000 people live in some 3,200 apartments. Designed by Harry Glück and others and built from 1973 to 1985, it consists of three rows of blocks, each about 400 m long. Rooftop and indoor pools, a shopping center, clinics, schools, and more are all within the estate, and underground parking and passageways connect the blocks. Its long, fluorescent-lit corridors have a stillness, until someone passes by, as though they had forgotten where they lead. This is where residents live their daily lives, so please be considerate when photographing or entering the common areas.'),
  ('JNp6eaQC', 'beae54ab7ab1810989f9eb32416402a9', 'Completed in 1989 and consecrated by Pope John Paul II the following year. Modeled on St. Peter''s Basilica, its dome rises 158 m, and the interior is said to hold about 18,000 people (7,000 seated), with room for 300,000 in the square in front; Guinness World Records lists it as the largest church in the world. It was built by Félix Houphouët-Boigny, Côte d''Ivoire''s first president, in his hometown of Yamoussoukro. At ordinary Masses most of the vast interior sits empty, and light from the stained glass drifts across the unoccupied pews.'),
  ('JsXsWk9W', '388b011977a2101b943b5d3e7909f3a6', 'The Metropolitan Area Outer Underground Discharge Channel, one of the largest underground floodwater channels in the world. In its huge surge tank, 22 m below ground, 59 pillars, each 18 m tall and weighing 500 tons, stand in rows, and this solemn sight has earned it the name "Underground Temple." It is a national flood control facility that drains water from the surrounding rivers into the Edo River during typhoons and other heavy rain.'),
  ('JtXE5uD5', 'f798d5f902a192e6e3e9a4b82e775388', 'An artificial waterfront park in Toyama, opened in 1997 on the site of an old canal boat basin. At each end of Tenmon Bridge, completed in 1999, stands an observation tower about 20 m tall, and the two towers are linked by a "red string telephone" made of fishing line. At night or early in the morning, when only the bridge lights are reflected on the deserted water, there is a stillness like a stage set up for someone and then forgotten.'),
  ('K4q9HiRe', '40e8dc27fe9cb52bcd1502ae8168402c', 'A single brick chimney stands among the farmland of Sakuraoka, Higashiasahikawa-cho, Asahikawa. It is what remains of the Yoshimura Brick Factory, founded in 1941, which also fired bricks for military use during the war but went out of business after demand fell in the postwar years. The factory buildings are already gone, leaving only the chimney. Its surface is noticeably cracked, so please do not go near it or enter the surrounding farmland; view it only from the road.'),
  ('K9KD56uc', '1cdb86bb485f83be1c6f8fc952a3a451', 'The Mashu Maru, a ferry that entered service in 1965 and linked Aomori and Hakodate until the Seikan ferry route was discontinued in March 1988. It is still moored at a quay near Hakodate Station and is open to the public as a memorial museum. Rows of seats, the wheelhouse, the radio room. Inside a ship that will never sail again, the time after its final voyage goes on just as it was.'),
  ('KM5aKcco', '3a1bf4f276f1e50c60625606626aed86', 'The passage to the Keiyo and Musashino Line platforms, an unusually long underground corridor even by the standards of Tokyo Station. It came about because the Keiyo Line, when it opened in 1990, took over a deep underground site far from the other platforms that had originally been planned for a Narita Shinkansen station. The long corridor, lined with moving walkways, goes on and on, with an atmosphere like another world. Users have described it as "like a hidden route in Dragon Quest" and "hard to believe it''s Tokyo Station": a hidden, otherworldly space tucked inside a huge terminal.'),
  ('KewaKgjE', 'dc8b4caac8b1b33b30cd020daa58f111', 'An elevated highway about 3.5 km long running through central São Paulo, opened in 1971. Its official name was changed in 2016 to Elevado Presidente João Goulart. The roadway passes right in front of apartment windows, only a few meters from the balconies. On weekday nights from 8 p.m. to 7 a.m., and on weekends and holidays, it is closed to cars and becomes a wide floor for people on foot. The city plans to end car traffic by 2029. The buildings along it are homes, so please take care not to photograph into the windows.'),
  ('Khnt6TG9', '88e880d8d1714bdf6f72b2568092cbad', 'The remains of a power plant on the banks of the Waga River in Sennin, Waga-cho, Kitakami, near Wagasennin Station on the JR Kitakami Line. It began operating in 1940 as a private power plant supplying electricity for ironmaking. It drew water from the Oarasawa Dam upstream, but when the Yuda Dam was completed in 1964, that dam sank beneath the new lake and the plant''s role came to an end. The white concrete powerhouse and the surge tank still remain. It can only be reached by crossing the river, and there are large holes in the floor, so do not try to get close.'),
  ('LVa5rVA7', 'a890995fe1abef6fb0158c861edc5cb5', 'An art museum in Atami that opened in 1982. From the entrance to the main building at the top of the hill, visitors ride a series of eight escalators up a height difference of 48.5 m. The long escalator passage, where the color of the light keeps changing, and the underground dome about 20 m across partway up form a space where you move on and on through light without seeing where you are headed.'),
  ('LXxqQyrL', '4cc4c98a740c2ffc517f21ed1513b9a0', 'The site of Makimine Station on the Takachiho Railway, which was cut off along its entire length by typhoon damage in 2005 and closed in 2008. The platform, waiting benches, and station sign remain as they were, though the tracks have been removed. The seats lined up on the green, overgrown platform quietly recall the everyday days when people sat here waiting for trains.'),
  ('Lmb9MUUi', '759c034e752aaedf2f10f8c342f1a5a9', 'A road tunnel about 24.5 km long linking Lærdal and Aurland in western Norway, opened in 2000. It is said to be the longest road tunnel in the world. Along the monotonous drive, which takes about 20 minutes, there are three large rock caverns, and only these are lit with blue light and yellow light along the edges. After driving on through a gray tube, a blue cave suddenly opens up ahead. From September 2026, for about two years, the tunnel will be closed every night from 6 p.m. to 6 a.m. for renovation work.'),
  ('Lw7vXA3V', '0a924d859f7124e772a1df4d32512390', 'The oldest terminal at Paris Charles de Gaulle Airport, opened in 1974. Designed by the architect Paul Andreu, it is a circular concrete building about 190 meters across, with an open atrium through its center where escalators enclosed in transparent tubes cross one another in midair. It is linked to the surrounding satellite boarding buildings by underground passages. A major renovation was carried out through 2023. At times when transfer passengers are few, it feels like being left behind inside a giant machine.'),
  ('MTwmuvAh', '3320e4791b52a42cec5fcd5dd9388661', 'A covered shopping street about 300 meters long in the Koza area of Okinawa City. In the mid-1970s it became the first street in Okinawa to get an arcade roof, and in the 1960s and 70s it was the busiest commercial district in central Okinawa. From the 1990s onward, closed shops became more and more noticeable, and today new businesses alternate with shuttered storefronts. The quiet under the long roof feels like an echo of its livelier days.'),
  ('MgcgYpSc', '5cf83d817794a03568b5bdeaeb6e05db', 'A white building on the slopes of Mt. Maya in Kobe, sometimes called the "Queen of Ruins." Around 1929-30, the company that ran the Maya Cable Car built it as the Maya Club, and after postwar renovations it became the Maya Kanko Hotel. Damage from typhoons and other disasters ended its hotel business around 1967; it was then used as a lodge for student training camps and has stood unused since 1993. In 2021 it became a Registered Tangible Cultural Property of Japan. It is badly deteriorated and off-limits, but the exterior can be seen from near Niji Station on the Maya Cable Car (whose subtitle means "in front of the former Maya Kanko Hotel"). The interior and its immediate surroundings can be visited only on paid guided tours that support its preservation.'),
  ('MkEEtUFS', '4f06b451cbfc35a40e2b82f9d6088cc2', 'A park built on a former garbage landfill, following a master plan by the sculptor Isamu Noguchi that treats "the entire park as one sculpture." It had its grand opening in 2005. Artificial hills, a glass pyramid, straight rows of trees and broad lawns: only geometric forms, set out beneath a wide sky. At quiet hours it looks like a stage that someone prepared and then forgot.'),
  ('MoLLMoNd', 'ff745cefac372339309e5ce9b1ce5531', 'The remains of a five-story hotel for skiers, standing on a hill above Route 17 in Ishiuchi, Minamiuonuma. With arched windows, an external spiral staircase and a tower with a pointed roof, it looks like a castle. It is said to have opened in the 1980s and appears to have closed in the mid-2000s. A later fire reportedly burned part of it, and the back of the building looks as if demolition stopped partway through. There is a risk of collapse, so please stay off the grounds and view it only from the highway or other public roads.'),
  ('Mvs56E7i', '2fbe9149c1403c48ba917f1129582da0', 'A spa and resort town founded in the 1920s on the shore of the salt lake Epecuén. At its peak around 1,500 people lived there, and 25,000 visitors came over the summer. In 1985 a dike failed, the lake overflowed, and the town sank beneath 7 to 10 meters of salt water. When the water began to recede in the late 2000s, only the frames of buildings were left, bleached white by salt. Salt-crusted walls look like bare bones, dead street trees still stand, and the street grid alone is traced on the ground just as it was. There are no roofs, only the outlines of rooms. The shape of the town survives, yet the life that once filled it has been taken away entirely.'),
  ('NJjytELM', 'db7b5b39b7424b00839f9d8f8c0b2ed6', 'The only swimming beach in Naha, right in the middle of the city. It is an artificial beach built in 1991; an elevated road crosses the sea just in front of it, and Naminoue Shrine stands on the rocks above. In the early morning or at night, with no swimmers around, this southern island beach hemmed in by an overpass and concrete looks somehow staged, like a set.'),
  ('NMmShzG7', 'b048e9964cf3c6c4949d626a294076fe', 'An unfinished high-rise condominium in the Sathorn district of central Bangkok, near the Chao Phraya River. Construction began in 1990 and stopped in 1997, when funding ran out during the Asian financial crisis. The skeleton, 49 floors including the basement and about 185 meters tall, is decorated in a neoclassical style with many columns and balconies, and windowless openings line it all the way to the top. Hotels and new condominiums now stand around it, and beside them the gray tower remains, frozen in time. The owner prohibits entry, so please view it only from outside.'),
  ('NP7tVnY3', 'b79d681af5acae0de9fe59a90ac835c4', 'A resort village recreating a Mediterranean fishing village, with white-walled, terracotta-roofed buildings crowded onto a hillside overlooking Ago Bay. It has been open since 1993, and gives you the illusion of strolling through a Greek or Spanish harbor town without leaving Japan. Cobblestone slopes, blue doors and towers modeled on bell towers are packed together into a scene that hardly seems to belong to a real Japanese coastline, and the sense of unreality is strongest at dusk, when the visitors thin out.'),
  ('NbGc4Sdz', 'f8e1700f240714e591d5e50c0dd377c2', 'A waterside promenade and a series of plazas running north to south through the center of Rokko Island, an artificial island. It was once lively with many shops, but at some times of day hardly anyone is around. Along the near-empty walkways and plazas, traces of its heyday sit side by side with stillness, and it has drawn attention as a liminal space with a strange, frozen-in-time atmosphere.'),
  ('Ngoqp3Rw', '296b5f1c2bc90fb8af91937a286f8263', 'The remains of a copper smelter that operated in the Meiji era. It shut down after only about ten years, leaving huge red-brick chimneys and the walls of the smelting facilities standing on the island, rising from a desolate landscape that overlooks the Seto Inland Sea. Today the site is preserved and used as part of the Inujima Seirensho Art Museum, where an industrial ruin and contemporary art come together to create a distinctive sense of time.'),
  ('NypNZ4hq', '794b9693859f7f5994b8004f15bf0786', 'The wooden building of the former Kaigake Elementary School in Kaigake, Hino, Shiga, built in 1930. After the school closed in 2001, a local nonprofit has preserved it as "Shakunage Gakko" (Rhododendron School) and opened it to the public. As you walk through classrooms with faded blackboards, old desks and textbooks from the period, the only sound echoing down the hall is the creak of the wooden floor. It has also been a filming location for many TV dramas. It is open on Saturdays, Sundays and holidays from 10:00 to 16:00 and closed on weekdays. A contribution of 300 yen toward its upkeep is required.'),
  ('PHMGPkY3', 'c29068c9487affa25199c79c6fe7b955', 'A covered shopping arcade behind Kokusai-dori in Naha, running from Ichiba-hondori toward the Kainan area. It has been around since the 1950s, and its arcade roof was added in 1988. Step one street away from the tourist crowds and you find closed shutters, old signs and small shops dotted along under fluorescent lights, as if you had wandered into the back side of a tourist town.'),
  ('PJh7ASjj', '6742a996ff390bf6394a39514ea6422b', 'A straight underground pedestrian space, about 520 meters long, linking JR Sapporo Station and Odori. It opened in March 2011. Open areas line both sides of a 12-meter-wide passage, and white columns and light falling from skylights repeat at even intervals as far as the eye can see. In the quiet early morning, all that is left is a wide corridor with no end in sight.'),
  ('PgKZ8Gxs', '73b4482960a589dd646a82ec07455a1d', 'A road of about 5 km built on an embankment across shallow sea, linking the Yokatsu Peninsula in Uruma with Henza Island. It opened in 1972. Partway along this single road, with nothing but sky and shallow water on either side, a large parking lot and the Ayahashi-kan roadside "sea station" sit alone as if afloat. On a quiet weekday evening, it feels like driving on and on down a road that leads nowhere.'),
  ('QAKdeuaZ', 'a1683ab42c10b50049a008e62173aa26', 'A village on a hilltop in the province of Matera. Tombs from the 8th century BC have been found here, and its name appears in a document from 1060. A landslide in 1963 led residents to begin relocating, and after a flood in 1972 and an earthquake in 1980, the old town was left completely empty. Its residents have moved to a new settlement at the foot of the hill. The surrounding land is a sparsely vegetated, eroded terrain known as calanchi, and the tower and stone houses remain stacked on the summit. The old town can be entered only with an authorized guide, wearing a helmet.'),
  ('QLHdXHVt', 'e1a308eedf111f7eb9ba52dde4abdaa0', 'A Seikan ferry that entered service in 1964 and linked Aomori and Hakodate until March 1988. It is now moored at the quay right next to Aomori Station and open to the public as a memorial ship. On the vehicle deck, where whole trains were once loaded aboard, retired passenger cars and freight cars still sit in rows, and there is also an exhibit that recreates the Showa-era area in front of Aomori Station with life-size figures. Walking through a ship that will never set sail again feels like wandering into a moment where time has stopped.'),
  ('QLwfbM4b', '2005ff1045b398b4561d5c7b38e7b105', 'The remains of a two-story wooden branch school in the hills of Aioi, Hachiman-cho, Gujo. It opened in 1909, was rebuilt at its current location in 1954 and closed in 1970. After closing, it served for a while as a community hall. One memorable detail is the staircase, where each step is labeled with a place value such as "one million" or "ten million." The first floor is still used for storing materials, so people come and go, and the floors and the building itself are badly deteriorated, so please do not go inside and view it only from outside.'),
  ('QedBsTL4', '6b13761dce4c3044740b912afb49fb91', 'Opened in 2006. Elevated east-west platforms and underground north-south platforms cross each other, and the five levels between them are pierced by a single atrium. Light from the glass vault at the top passes down through layer after layer of steel and escalators to the platforms 15 meters below ground. Looking down from the railings, you see floor after floor stacked up with their centers open, and people moving up and down inside glass cylinders. In a lull between trains, standing at the bottom of this vertical shaft, the only thing that becomes clear is that you are on an in-between level, belonging neither "above" nor "below." A vertical waiting room where the words "arrival" and "departure" lose their meaning.'),
  ('Qom26yXL', '36b6f5d25070bf322a75527b7b35ad7b', 'An elevator that climbs diagonally up the slope of Minamiyamate in Nagasaki. It was built in 2002 as part of a city road, and anyone can ride it free of charge. A car with round windows rises quietly, looking down over the harbor and the houses on the hillside. Neither quite a road nor quite a ride, it is a curious passage found only in a city of slopes.'),
  ('QxTyYMA9', 'f1da7048f22219b66644bc74d04a0139', 'The site of a Wild West-themed amusement park near Kinugawa Onsen. It began in 1973 as Kinugawa Family Bokujo (Kinugawa Family Ranch) and became Western Village in 1982, drawing crowds with its frontier-town streets, a replica of Mount Rushmore, and a steam locomotive brought over from the United States. It has been closed since December 2006 and has never reopened. The grounds are watched by an electronic security system and cannot be entered. Please view the entrance buildings only from the road.'),
  ('RAi2rKjn', '22302ab26f5f1596b324a2cdd7c6f528', 'A Western-style cave dwelling carved into a rocky hill in Yoshimi, Saitama, by a farmer named Takahashi Minekichi, who dug it with chisels and other hand tools for 21 years, from 1904 until his death in 1925. Its official name is Gankutsu Hotel Kosokan, but it never operated as lodging; the word ''hotel'' is said to come from onlookers who watched him work and remarked that he was ''digging a cave'' (gankutsu hotteru). His son carried on the work and sightseers came to visit, but parts collapsed in typhoons in 1982 and 1987 and it was closed. It is now fenced off and entry is prohibited. Part of the exterior can be seen from the shop across the road, which also displays photographs from its active years.'),
  ('RKcSpTm3', '10496ba40f7d627560e8afcc91632426', 'An art museum next to the Sannai-Maruyama archaeological site, opened in 2006. Designed by Jun Aoki, the building looks as if white boxes had been set down over earthen trenches like those at an excavation. White-walled corridors and stairways interweave, and as you walk you gradually lose track of where you are. On snowy days and at night, the white building itself seems to float apart from reality.'),
  ('Rn4ZaCs9', 'd37213f3e0da5857b2fa543011c81e56', 'A ''star house'', an apartment building with an unusual plan that places units in three directions, built in the late 1950s and early 1960s when Japan''s large housing estates were being developed. Seen from above it is shaped like a star (or a Y), and with the stairwell at its center every unit becomes a corner unit, a product of what was then progressive design thinking. Several of these buildings still stand at Tokiwadaira Danchi, and looking up through the geometric stairwell you see an asymmetrical triangular ceiling, a space unlike any other. Visited by housing estate enthusiasts from all over Japan, it is a liminal space born of Showa-era residential architecture. People still live here, so please be considerate and do not point your camera at homes or residents.'),
  ('RpUyExpq', 'ccdce5d3eb964716a64d46fbb68bb0b4', 'A village known in Greek as Levissi, once home to several thousand Greek Orthodox Christians. Its residents left in the 1923 population exchange between Greece and Turkey, and the village was left on the hillside, almost uninhabited ever since. An earthquake in 1957 damaged many of the houses as well. Around 500 roofless stone houses are stacked up along the slope, and churches still stand among them. It is now protected as a historic site, and you can walk through it after paying an entrance fee. Many of the walls are crumbling, so watch your step.'),
  ('RuCpHWym', '876da12df6830128f62fd2c670ece3c2', 'The site of a former ski area at the foot of Mt. Akazura in Nishigo, Fukushima. It was originally the Akazurayama Ski Area; in the late 1990s it replaced its lifts and center house and changed its name, but it appears to have stopped operating around 2000. The slopes have returned to grassland, and a 2019 visitor''s account described a lodge building with broken windows still standing. The site has been closed to entry since 2021, so please view it only from the Mt. Akazura hiking trail or the surrounding roads.'),
  ('Rxz5y2My', '2db9367d843aaec6da623cca464a01c1', 'A residential complex in Quarry Bay, Hong Kong, made up of five buildings including Yick Cheong Building and Montane Mansion. Built in the 1960s, the blocks are joined in an E shape around long, narrow courtyards. The windows, air conditioners and laundry of about 2,200 flats cover every wall, and looking up, the sky shrinks to a thin strip. Step into the courtyard and the street noise fades, leaving only the sounds of daily life drifting down from above. Appearances in films brought a rise in visitors coming to take photos, and in 2018 signs went up prohibiting unauthorized photography. Some 10,000 people live here, so please take care not to disturb the residents.'),
  ('SAqp6opk', '6675b69cf3d66adc5df25a9421d12caa', 'A 143.6-meter glass tower at the edge of Akita Port. It opened in 1994, and its observation room 100 meters above the ground has been open free of charge since 2007. At night and on weekdays the room is nearly empty, with only the Sea of Japan and the lights of the harbor spreading out beyond the windows. An empty room high in the air that still holds the atmosphere of the early Heisei years.'),
  ('SdUSWAUL', '3bfb559cf7580488d67ebc9d25f90c4d', 'A station completely rebuilt in 2014. Its sharply pointed metal roof juts out over the square to the south, toward the city center. Inside, the ceiling of the hall is covered with a lattice of thin wooden slats, and beyond it a glass roof covers the platforms in one continuous span. Light falling through the glass is cut into fine pieces by the wooden lattice and always casts the same pattern on the floor. Because the glass-walled hall opens straight onto the square, there is no clear moment of having entered the station. In the quiet before the first trains, only the lattice pattern stays still. A roof that is neither arrival nor departure, made for a city that is simply passed through, over and over.'),
  ('SrKMLupa', '747530b0019645ddac5798a7a21c8cb0', 'An underground shopping street that runs in a cross shape beneath the Kamiyacho intersection in central Hiroshima. It opened in 2001. Before the shops open and after they close, the long passages and plazas, with only their bright lights left on, become an underground space with no clear destination.'),
  ('SxcZ69LR', '3f53209a803c7a0a76a6c3cf11421e0b', 'A cylindrical residential high-rise in the Berea district of Johannesburg, South Africa. Completed in 1975, it stands about 173 meters tall. A circular atrium runs up through the center of the building, and looking up from the bottom, identical rows of windows rise in rings all the way to the sky. During the building''s decline in the 1990s, rubbish is said to have piled up several stories high at the bottom of this atrium. After renovation it is once again home to many people, so please do not go inside without permission, and be considerate of the residents'' daily lives.'),
  ('TamekAJY', '812b429e749d23f4b649ea925c924a52', 'The remains of a large nine-story lodging facility standing among the farmland of Meiwa, Mie. It is said to have opened in 1985 as a fee-paying retirement home and to have been converted into a hotel when not enough residents moved in. The hotel in turn appears to have closed in the 2000s, and the building, with its many rows of windows, still stands as it was in the middle of the rice fields. It is a privately owned building, so please stay off the grounds and view it only from the surrounding public roads.'),
  ('TcnQTVk5', '236b30aa82d35f694e526f6d256ac242', 'The site of Kumanotaira signal station (later a station), once a key point on the old Shin''etsu Main Line. Trains in both directions used to wait here for one another, and there were substation facilities as well. Today mossy platforms, the remains of the substation and the portals of disused tunnels still stand quietly, half buried in greenery. Wild animals pass along the old track bed, now little more than a game trail, a sight that makes you strongly feel the time that has piled up since people left.'),
  ('TdJmev6C', 'ba0ef112f718a6d079b8cd0597fd6324', 'Kinugawa Onsen, in Nikko, Tochigi. In this valley hot spring town, once crowded with tour groups, lies a landscape left behind by the fever of the bubble era.

Looking across the gorge from the bridge, you see closed hot spring inns crowded one above another on the opposite slope. Their windows are broken, the lettering on their signs has faded, and ivy is quietly creeping over their walls. The banquet halls and open-air baths that must once have been full of tour groups are now visited only by the wind that blows through the valley.

The sound of the Kinugawa River, the river mist rising in the morning, and the huge silent buildings on the far bank: prosperity and decline fit within the same frame here, a view particular to this place. The bridge itself is a public thoroughfare, and its greatest appeal is that you can take in the whole scene from a safe distance without setting foot in the ruins across the river.

That such a landscape exists right beside the glamorous image of Nikko as a tourist destination is itself a kind of liminality.

Please note
The former hot spring inns visible across the river are all abandoned buildings on private property. Entering them is trespassing and can lead to serious accidents such as falls. This spot is for enjoying the view from the bridge, so please never go onto the other side.'),
  ('TekzRCTj', '02df0bb1ea362bd61caf19cd2104d3ff', 'A building modeled on a European castle, standing on the summit of Mt. Sanpo in Konan, Kochi. It was built as an observation facility, together with an amusement park and a restaurant, to coincide with the opening of the Ryugado Skyline in 1973, and was later also used as a museum. It closed in the early 2000s, and even after the surrounding facilities were torn down, this building alone remained. It can be seen on the mountaintop from the direction of Kochi Ryoma Airport and is fondly regarded locally as a symbol of the town. The summit area has at times been closed off for construction, so please view it from the foot of the mountain or from the road.'),
  ('TivYALh3', '5756143204208e4ca927145d98f9aed1', 'A saucer-shaped monument built in 1981 on a mountaintop 1,441 meters above sea level, commemorating the 1891 gathering that marked the beginning of Bulgaria''s socialist movement. Inside the building, about 60 meters across, is a circular main hall whose walls and ceiling were covered with about 937 square meters of mosaics. After the change of regime in 1989, upkeep stopped, the roof was torn open, and the mosaics were damaged by wind and rain. Conservation work is now under way and entry to the interior is prohibited. Please view it only from outside.'),
  ('TmTsHg39', '8e1aedb5277502047b513f7786ed6041', 'The remains of a small branch school on Komagahara, a plateau about 1,000 meters above sea level in Shitara, Aichi. It was set up for the children of a settlement founded after the war by people who had returned from Manchuria and cleared the virgin forest, and its single-story wooden school building was built in 1953. Classes were suspended in 1978, and the school was formally closed in 1982. The building is badly deteriorated. A campsite lies next door and people come and go, so please do not enter the building and view it quietly from outside. In winter, take care with snow and ice.'),
  ('ULU2BB7S', '45b04cdd80a821c0aba510f9277788b2', 'A component of a World Cultural Heritage site, where a group of Meiji-era coal mine buildings survives almost as it was. The red-brick winding engine house and a huge headframe stand quietly in an open yard, steeped in the taut stillness peculiar to industrial facilities that have stopped working. As a well-maintained historic site, it is also easy to visit.'),
  ('UNSoSSWk', 'cb6a9ad63ac0dfb2a4a7d11d74721e32', 'An English-style town completed around 2006 as part of ''One City, Nine Towns'', a plan to develop the suburbs of Shanghai. On a site of about one square kilometer stand cobbled streets, rows of Victorian-style houses, red telephone boxes and a church modeled on one in Bristol. It was planned for 10,000 residents, but for many years its population has stayed in the 2,000s. Tourists and wedding photo crews come to the center, while the residential blocks are closed off behind gates, and the farther down the streets you go, the fainter the sense of people becomes. A townscape that resembles somewhere, yet is nowhere.'),
  ('UTtg7ouu', 'b6ad805d83a7d87bfc0f1bdab213049b', 'An unfinished hotel on Mt. Ojigatake in Tamano, Okayama, with seven floors above ground and two below. It was the first project under a national program to build facilities in national and quasi-national parks, funded with about 4 billion yen in government loans under the Resort Act, and the building was completed in 1993. But after the bubble economy collapsed, money for the interior work could not be raised, and it was abandoned without ever receiving a single guest. In 2006 it was sold at auction into private hands. Its windows have been smashed and it is covered in graffiti. The site is off-limits and barricaded, so please view it only from the rooftop observation deck of the Ojigatake rest house.'),
  ('UUa8yY66', 'f7de1c07dfd6c0cb51541fa2bab220c4', 'A thermal bath designed by Peter Zumthor and completed in 1996. It is built from about 60,000 slabs of locally quarried Vals gneiss laid in layers, and set half into the mountainside. Light falls through narrow gaps in the ceiling, and small pools of water at different temperatures are linked by stone passages. Apart from the large windows that open onto the valley, there is only stone, water and shadow, and you find yourself drifting through the water with no particular destination. Visitors not staying at the hotel must book in advance, and guests are asked not to take photographs inside the baths.'),
  ('UWm8ASTY', '93d1f5b928c934a8cd86df4adb289a5a', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Muroran Main Line. It sits in a gap of only about 80 meters between two long tunnels, surrounded by steep cliffs on three sides and by Uchiura Bay on the fourth. It was set up in 1943 as a signal station where trains could pass each other, and became a station in 1987. No road leads to the station, and only six local trains a day stop here. Its closure was considered in 2015, but from the following year the town of Toyoura has kept it running. Brown bears appear in the area, so avoid visiting alone.'),
  ('UZJT2Ht2', 'ebf9672dca0f414c420aba4c110fe0b9', 'A public facility on Jozenji-dori in Sendai, opened in 2001. Designed by Toyo Ito, its floors are held up only by 13 steel tubes that waver like seaweed. Seen through the glass, the stacked floors float above the street at night like a glowing aquarium. Anyone can come in for free until late in the evening, and on the quieter floors, wide spaces with no fixed purpose stretch out in silence.'),
  ('UdwmdZmu', 'f15ddd16c7d4ce3e8d39b4d7a0e95a4e', 'A high-rise in New Belgrade (Novi Beograd) whose official name is the Western City Gate of Belgrade. Designed by Mihajlo Mitrović, it was completed at the end of the 1970s. A two-level bridge joins its two towers, one residential and one for offices, and on top sits a restaurant that was meant to revolve. It was planned as a gate marking the entrance to the city along the road from the airport into Belgrade. It was designated a cultural monument in 2021. People still live in the residential tower, so please be considerate of the residents.'),
  ('UeKBsfTf', '1c3c2e6ee11d7ad10fe2a0ae4c107e3b', 'An Art Deco railway station that opened in 1929. It has a tower about 82 meters tall, and when it opened, 200 trains a day arrived and departed. As rail travel declined it fell out of use, and with its last train in October 1979 its life as a station came to an end. It then lay neglected for many years, but a preservation group took it over, and repair work on the vaulted concourse is now under way, with the aim of reopening in 2027. The building cannot be entered during construction, and events are held on the lawn outside.'),
  ('UsmTFajm', '1bd458e6fcd15a29dfd779943ea36d08', 'The Oculus is the main hall of the World Trade Center Transportation Hub in Manhattan, New York City. Designed by the Spanish architect Santiago Calatrava, it opened on March 3, 2016. Its total cost reached about 4 billion dollars, and it is sometimes called the most expensive railway station in the world. White steel members like ribs rise from both sides to frame a skylight about 100 meters long, and Calatrava compared the form to a bird released from a child''s hands. The interior is made of nothing but white marble and white steel, with almost no clues of color or time. It is a vast junction where the PATH line, 11 subway lines and an underground shopping mall come together, yet late at night or early in the morning, when the crowds thin out, only the white skeleton remains, an unreal space.'),
  ('V6z9Xx58', 'd616e224004703c5b2b6b35ed7d83ef0', 'A shopping street inside a housing estate that keeps a strong flavor of the Showa era, hard to believe within Tokyo''s 23 wards. It lies at the heart of the Toei Kirigaoka housing estate, with old-fashioned independent shops lined up under the wavy roof of an arcade. In recent years empty shops have become noticeable, and even on weekday afternoons there are few people about, leaving a stillness as if time had stopped. Though close to central Tokyo, it has an odd, floating feel, as if you had wandered into another era, and it has reportedly been used as a music video location. The shopping street''s cooperative was dissolved in March 2025, and a new commercial facility is planned on the neighboring land as the estate is rebuilt. Because it sits inside a public housing estate where people still live, please take care not to capture homes or residents when taking photos.'),
  ('VE2WnRAp', 'b87f606ace64a8b1d19d92918266d729', 'A bus station in Preston, in northwest England. It opened in October 1969, designed by Building Design Partnership (BDP) with structural engineering by Ove Arup. White curved concrete wraps the edges of the multi-storey car park above, creating layer upon layer of long horizontal lines. It survived proposals for demolition, was listed at Grade II in 2013, and reopened in 2018 after renovation. Between departures, the long waiting concourse is lined with nothing but identical doors and benches.'),
  ('VVXMdSMV', '1cc00cadb8311940757b49a88e8b5f15', 'A large residential complex in the City of London, in the heart of the capital, designed by Chamberlin, Powell and Bon and built between 1965 and 1976. It holds about 2,000 homes, and its three high-rise towers were the tallest residential buildings in London when completed. Elevated walkways called the "highwalks," which separate pedestrians from the road traffic at ground level, wind between the buildings on several levels; because it is easy to get lost, yellow lines are painted on the ground to guide the way. People live here, so please take care not to photograph their homes or the residents'' gardens.'),
  ('VahpLYw4', '050dd61a9ad8884ef0e59fbd0dbf3e6f', 'A red-brick building built as a courthouse in 1922 and designated an Important Cultural Property of Japan. It served as a court until 1979 and is now open to the public free of charge as the Nagoya City Archives. A central staircase of marble and stained glass, a reconstructed courtroom, and long corridors lined with identical doors. On quiet weekdays, only the stillness of a building whose work is done fills the halls.'),
  ('VjHQSWFR', '631da340532c754adb37979b955aaad4', 'The former Yokota Clinic, a wooden hospital building completed in 1926 (Taisho 15) and now nearly 100 years old, still standing as it slowly decays. Medical instruments in the examination room, medicine cabinets, and chairs in the waiting room have been left just as they were, creating a space where time seems to have stopped. Today it is used as a photo studio and event venue, hosting events such as ghost-story nights, and it is a spot known among liminal space enthusiasts.'),
  ('VwXjTi86', 'a0c475a8645142a0bb248e6103858a5d', 'An underground passage spreading beneath the vast pedestrian deck that links Shimbashi Station with the high-rise towers of Shiodome. In contrast to the glass skyscrapers above, it has an impersonal atmosphere of concrete and artificial light. People do pass through, yet the bustle above ground feels strangely far away, and at certain times of day only a few figures can be seen. Although it is a rational space designed for an office district, its featureless junctions and narrow corridors combine to create an unreal atmosphere. It has drawn attention on social media as a liminal space hidden in the everyday life of a central Tokyo business district.'),
  ('W69E6kQM', '22e3ec14f68f0b131222e69f79eb7a99', 'The remains of the Senjaku Ski Area on the slopes of Mt. Teine in Teine Ward, Sapporo. It opened around the time of the 1972 Sapporo Olympics and connected near the top of the mountain with the Shirakaba area of Teine Olympia. Left out of the lift upgrades of the 1990s, it has not operated since 1999. Lift towers, loading platforms, and a half-collapsed rest house still remain in the woods, and hikers pass by in both summer and winter. Please keep away from the crumbling buildings and view them only from the hiking trail.'),
  ('W9n7DymW', '5a29183f0ff6a44c18bc7efd50cfa407', 'A multi-tenant building on the west side of Ikebukuro that strongly preserves the atmosphere of the 1970s. It opened in 1968 as an all-round entertainment building, and a movie theater (Cinema Rosa), a bowling alley, a game arcade, a live music venue, and restaurants still operate inside. The interior is marked by the dim lighting and tangled corridors typical of Showa-era amusement buildings, and the stairs leading underground and the retro lettering on its signs create a distinctive mood. Often introduced on social media as "retro" or "otherworldly," it is a space that seems left behind by time, a liminal space where you can feel outside the everyday right in the middle of the city.'),
  ('Wqx5BmKq', '7ee0a9bb955057751388931d961bd51b', 'The ruins of a smelting plant in the woods near Mutsu Bay in Ominato, Mutsu, Aomori. Nippon Tokushu Kokan opened it in 1938 to make iron from iron sand gathered on the Shimokita Peninsula, but production fell short of plans. It was handed over to the navy in 1943 and appears to have been left unused after the war. Its large, crumbling concrete buildings and chimney are buried among the trees, and because many bats live inside, it is also known as "Komori Yashiki" (the Bat Mansion). The structure is steadily collapsing, so please do not go inside and view it only from outside.'),
  ('X8fbHEwM', '8bf59d8389aa2025d9564ed9750ad540', 'A 100-meter red steel tower standing on Hakata Pier. It opened in 1964 and was designed by Tachu Naito, who also designed Tokyo Tower. Anyone can go up to its round observation room 70 meters above the ground for free, and at quiet times a Showa-era room seems to float silently there, surrounded by the lights of the harbor.'),
  ('XAyRF28R', '6542a3543ecdad63f65242b1a60dcf2a', 'A 50-unit apartment complex on a seaside cliff in Calpe, eastern Spain, completed in 1973 to a design by Ricardo Bofill. Its design takes cues from the kasbahs of North Africa, with exterior walls painted in shades from red to pink and the courtyards and stairs in blue and violet. Stairs and walkways fold back on one another again and again, so wherever you climb you arrive at a similar landing framed by a cut-out patch of sky, and in photos it can look almost like flat graphic art. Since around 2019 the grounds have been limited to residents and guests, and general visits are not possible. Please enjoy it only from outside.'),
  ('XBXoFcN3', '84cf3fe8c9771c6c223624403a146d61', 'A large complex directly connected to Shin-Kobe Station, made up of a hotel, shops, and a theater. Its commercial zone was renamed "Kotonohako Kobe" in 2019 and is still open today. Although it sits right in front of the station, depending on the time of day its wide corridors and atriums can be almost empty of people, giving the feeling that something out of the ordinary lies right alongside everyday life.'),
  ('XroRJy6m', '01b0dae41bff847acbc6350e2d14b27d', 'The remains of a hydroelectric power plant in Sasamado, Kawane-cho, Shimada, standing in a tea field on the far side of a sweeping bend of the Oi River. It began operating in 1931 as a power plant for the paper company Tokai Shiryo (later Tokai Pulp), later passed to Chubu Electric Power, and was decommissioned in 1961. Its ivy-covered concrete building stands on the sloping tea field as if cut off from the surrounding villages. It can only be reached by crossing the river, and since it is private property that is still being managed, please view it only from the opposite bank.'),
  ('XzQcU8bA', '99ab8f3bedceda6ea58f6868b5822275', 'The remains of a concrete plant at the foot of the Shin-Minori Bridge on Prefectural Route 21 in Minami Ward, Okayama. It appears to have ceased operations around the early 2000s, and it was used as a filming location for the 2015 film "Fugawarii," made by an Okayama film production group. Large plant equipment streaked with rust towers just below the bridge and can be seen even from car windows while waiting at the traffic light. The site is private property, so please view it only from the bridge or public roads.'),
  ('Y2ciMqaA', 'e2a6000686f83b36a421dae6e92be1e4', 'Nebukawa Station, with its wooden station building more than 100 years old, stands on high ground overlooking Sagami Bay. An unstaffed station chosen as one of the "100 Best Stations of Kanto," it offers a sweeping view of the Pacific horizon from the platform. It also carries a tragic history: in the Great Kanto Earthquake of 1923, a train at the station was swept into the sea together with the platform by a landslide. Today it feels like a station where time has stopped, with only a quiet, empty waiting room and the sea breeze passing through.'),
  ('Y2m7YAaB', 'a5f4143feb49cbee6649940c12da257e', 'A ceramics complex that opened in 2002 in a valley among the hills of Tajimi. Designed by Arata Isozaki & Associates with Kumagai Architectural Design Office, its massive buildings clad in stone and tile, along with plazas and long walkways, spread out along the valley. On days without events there is hardly anyone around, and it has the stillness of a ruin where only the buildings have been left behind.'),
  ('YfVjS3B4', '003c0baab6a26a77b512a4f3b6be6a72', 'The remains of employee housing at the Matsuo Mine, once called "Paradise Above the Clouds." In the decade from the mid-1950s (the Showa 30s), when it flourished as what was called the largest sulfur mine in the East, as many as 13,000 people lived here, but after the mine closed in 1969 (Showa 44) every resident left and the buildings became empty ruins. The reinforced concrete apartment blocks standing at regular intervals in the wilderness create a strange sight that resembles ancient ruins or an abandoned European castle, and their silhouette against the backdrop of Mt. Iwate is known as an unreal landscape.'),
  ('YktDGVfG', 'f98c3172ccb2d8383ecdb839d61c8b71', 'The remains of a ryokan in Yunoko Onsen, Minamata, Kumamoto, which advertised its 30-meter cave bath as "the best cave bath in Japan." With 71 rooms it was one of the largest inns in the hot spring town, but it ceased operations in April 2015. It still stands by the sea in its unusual form: a five-story wooden old wing, said to have been built in the 1950s, sandwiched between high-rise wings on both sides. Ivy has crept over it, and the roof has begun to sag. There are no-entry signs and security cameras at the entrance, so please view it only from nearby places such as the neighboring Yunoko Onsen Park.'),
  ('ZARd5zBd', 'c023d765e18b9ccafbe1021c2524b93b', 'The Takamatsu central shopping district, where eight shopping streets connect and covered arcades continue for a total of about 2.7 km. At its heart, at the northern end of Marugamemachi, is a plaza beneath a glass dome 32.2 m high. Early in the morning or at night, when the shop shutters are down, empty streets stretch on and on under the bright roofs, and the plaza beneath the large dome only makes the emptiness more striking.'),
  ('ZKgQrh3z', 'c9a21ce8c72af298efdb106097994264', 'The remains of the coal preparation plant of the Ikeshima Coal Mine, an undersea mine that operated as the last coal mine in Kyushu. After the mine closed in 2001 (Heisei 13), the facilities were left on the island as they were, and rusted corrugated roofs, pipes, and coal-washing machinery spread across the slope facing the sea, piled one upon another. Seen from the air, the complex structure looks like a single giant machine, giving it a strange presence as an industrial heritage site frozen in time.'),
  ('ZM9ZvdRe', '81248194d8b84d602a8f2583a27bd169', 'The remains of the Wake factory of Nippon Bengara Kogyo, in the mountains of Wake, Okayama. Founded in 1922, the company used pyrite ore from the Yanahara Mine to produce bengara, a red pigment. In 2000 its bengara business was transferred to Toda Kogyo, and this factory ceased operations. Its warehouses and refining equipment remain, their walls and ceilings still stained red with bengara. The site is still used by a private company and is off-limits, so please view it only from outside the fence.'),
  ('ZMkEuArd', '2d304e2c8d51dd8017bf8ed1a703e1d3', 'An island off Nagasaki Port that was once an undersea coal mining facility, called "Gunkanjima" (Battleship Island) because of its silhouette lined with high-rise buildings. When the mine closed in 1974, all of its residents left and it became uninhabited. The schools and housing where people once lived have been left to decay, giving off a mysterious atmosphere as if time had stopped, and it has drawn attention from around the world as a liminal space.'),
  ('ZPX5ynHL', 'ef04b719106229f776454a3eaaf6c10b', 'The former Yamagata Prefectural Office and Prefectural Assembly Hall, built in 1916. These brick buildings were designed by Shinnosuke Tahara and are a national Important Cultural Property. They were restored to their original appearance over about ten years starting in 1986 and are now open to the public free of charge. Along the long corridors lie the former governor''s office and meeting rooms with no one in their seats, and a stillness as if only their role as government offices had slipped away.'),
  ('ZSXELtqx', 'c9c4cc78c0d3c05c824a9e2ecd8a5e77', 'A fountain tower at the center of Tsuruma Park, which opened in 1909 as Nagoya''s first park. It was built the following year, in 1910, for the 10th Kansai Prefectural Union Exhibition held in the park. It was dismantled in 1973 for subway construction but restored to its original form in 1977, and it is now a cultural property of Nagoya City. Early in the morning, when no one is around, the circle of marble columns and the Western-style plaza look like the grounds of a finished exposition that have simply been left behind.'),
  ('ZUcjGgAk', 'f3b1f4d5f9d72075b8aac6ea53b876a0', 'A covered shopping street about 220 m long, east of Nagoya Station and leading into the Nagono district. The street dates back to the Edo period; its arcade was built in 1964 and renovated in 2015. It is lively during the day, but early in the morning before the shops open, or on closing days, it becomes a quiet passageway where only shutters and signs line up beneath the long arcade.'),
  ('ZXWghuAD', '11799f3f2cee164f78c85bf50f58acdc', 'The remains of a large four-story hospital built in 1987 in Itoigawa, then an area short of medical services, with funding from local citizens. With 114 general beds, it served as a core of local healthcare, including rehabilitation after strokes, but revisions to medical fees and a shortage of doctors led it to go bankrupt in 2007 with debts of about 2.2 billion yen, and it closed. It mattered so much to the area that Himekawa Station is even said to have been opened with the hospital in mind. Since it closed, broken windows and graffiti have spread, and it stands slowly decaying in full view along the national highway.'),
  ('Zi6c5ZgC', 'd75200632c12b3f3ed5ebfe0651aa85e', 'An abandoned village in the desert of the Emirate of Sharjah in the United Arab Emirates. Rows of houses and a mosque remain, said to have been built in the 1970s to settle nomadic people. The residents reportedly left by around the 1990s, after which sand dunes flowed into the houses. Thresholds are buried in sand, room floors have turned into slopes, and only the colors painted on the walls hint at the lives once lived here. Identical houses stand in rows, each sunk halfway into the sand in the same way. The buildings are deteriorating and could collapse, so watch your step and what is overhead, and come prepared for the daytime heat.'),
  ('ZoeUqjtb', '737f3488faa3e5e0fa21e86fbfc34ceb', 'The Line 11 platform at Arts et Métiers station on the Paris Métro, renovated in 1994 to mark the 200th anniversary of the Conservatoire national des arts et métiers, the institution behind the neighboring museum of arts and crafts. Its walls and ceiling are covered in copper plates studded with countless rivets, and round portholes line the side walls at regular intervals. Overhead, huge gears of unclear purpose are fixed in place, never turning. The design imitates the interior of Jules Verne''s Nautilus; the lighting is dimmed to a yellow glow, and the copper surfaces give back a dull reflection. Left alone in this tube after a train departs, you feel less like you are on a metro platform than like you have slipped aboard a ship diving somewhere. For a few minutes, a place meant for everyday transfers is replaced by an entirely different era and medium.'),
  ('aGkQCYCw', '78f8e0e05d824af4844ce73c67f97d37', 'An apartment building completed in 1975, designed by Ricardo Bofill and Taller de Arquitectura. More than 400 homes, made by stacking units of about 28 m², surround courtyards inside the building, linked by bridge-like walkways crossing above and below. The outer walls are red, and the inner courtyard walls are covered in blue tiles. Whether you look up or down, the same walkways and balconies keep repeating. People still live here, so visits to the interior are limited to reservation-only tours offered by the residents'' side.'),
  ('aMYSkmHZ', 'fdafef4571de619a3d2d01edf41b908a', 'A space-themed station on the Tashkent Metro, opened in 1984. The platform walls are covered in ceramic panels shading from deep to pale blue, lined with round ceramic medallions depicting figures from astronomy and space exploration such as Gagarin, Tereshkova, Ulugh Beg and Galileo. Linear lights and glass stars scattered across the ceiling suggest the Milky Way. On an empty platform just after a train has left, only the vision of the future painted in the Soviet era remains on the blue walls.'),
  ('aRBAGXS8', '457e0a81d2ce58800818e71c023631ee', 'A development on artificial islands reclaimed from the strait with Singapore, in Johor at the southern tip of Malaysia. Led by the major Chinese property developer Country Garden, it began selling units in the mid-2010s and promoted itself as a city for 700,000 people. As of 2023, however, fewer than 10,000 people were reported to live there, and many windows in the high-rise towers stay dark at night. Streets of well-kept paving, roadside trees and closed shops seem frozen, still waiting for people to arrive. Some people do live here, so please be considerate of residents when taking photos.'),
  ('ahMnVhLD', 'f1aae0222fcd2a48a0441afaffc110b7', 'Park Hill is a large housing estate on a hill in Sheffield, in the north of England, built between 1957 and 1961 to designs by Jack Lynn and Ivor Smith. Its defining feature is its "streets in the sky": elevated decks about 3 meters wide, meant to recreate in a high-rise the neighborliness of the alleys between old terraced houses. The decks run unbroken from block to block and were once wide enough for small milk floats to drive along. From the 1980s the estate declined and became notorious, but in 1998 it was listed at Grade II* (it is said to be the largest listed building in Europe), and since 2009 it has been undergoing phased renovation. The long decks, with the same railings and openings repeating endlessly, feel indoors even though they are outside: streets suspended in midair, cut off from the ground. People still live here, so please be careful not to point your camera at homes or residents.'),
  ('auXGVFFE', '6a8432f2a2d4a7f0ad5c97af67aeea63', 'The parliament building in Bucharest, Romania, formerly known as the House of the People. Construction began in 1984 on the orders of the Ceaușescu regime and continued until 1997. The lead architect was Anca Petrescu. With a floor area of about 365,000 square meters and around 1,100 rooms, it is said to be the heaviest building in the world. A large part of the old city was demolished to build it, and about 40,000 people were relocated. Marble halls and long corridors go on and on, and the interior can be visited on guided tours.'),
  ('b9ekkcic', 'cad03fa37448f84440d2a0891608ffba', 'The 100-meter tower of Art Tower Mito, which opened in 1990. Designed by Arata Isozaki, it takes a spiral form made by stacking equilateral triangular titanium panels in a twisting pattern. The observation room, reached by a glass elevator, sits 86.4 meters above the ground and is a small room holding just 19 people. Looking down on the city through its submarine-like windows, you feel as if you have been sealed inside a piece of geometry.'),
  ('bABcy5N8', '673e775269285d8a1e788b353576087f', 'An underground shopping street running about 590 meters beneath Tenjin in Fukuoka, opened in 1976. Inspired by 19th-century Europe, it has cobblestone-style floors and ironwork ceilings with arabesque patterns, and the lighting in the passages is deliberately kept dim. Before the shops open or after they close, the empty passages look like the alleys of a town that exists nowhere.'),
  ('bDmapNGx', '567594fc22c9eec6ad0b5eb2146aad9e', 'A housing complex in Noisy-le-Grand, in the eastern suburbs of Paris, designed by Ricardo Bofill and completed around 1982. It consists of three buildings, the Palacio, the Théâtre and the Arc, with precast concrete shaped into the columns and arches of classical architecture. Classical forms far larger than human scale surround a plaza like a stage set, and it has been used as a filming location for Brazil and The Hunger Games. Many people still live here, so please take care not to photograph residents or their homes.'),
  ('bRVna6ES', '16f5bf1574107045254f4dba275d9005', 'A small amusement park on the roof of the Matsuzakaya Takatsuki department store, in front of JR Takatsuki Station. It opened together with the store in 1979 and is said to be the last surviving department store rooftop amusement park in the Kansai region. Most rides, such as the go-karts and a small train running on rails, cost 100 yen, and admission is free. The old rides, whose parts have become hard to find, are kept running through ongoing repairs. Rooftop amusement parks were once found on department stores all over Japan, but only a few remain today. Closed on rainy days.'),
  ('bUpymfK9', 'b313a88ac7b97028285534e40ea8906b', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Tsurumi Line, between Tsurumi and Tsurumi-Ono stations. It opened in 1930 as a station of the Tsurumi Rinko Railway, and apart from the ticket gate being moved, it is said never to have been substantially rebuilt. Beneath the elevated tracks, a dim passage of repeating arches stretches on, and little outside light reaches it even during the day. The outer wall of the station building still bears bullet holes said to be from strafing in 1945. People still live and run shops beneath the tracks, so when taking photos, please take care not to capture residents or their doorways.'),
  ('bhAnQa6U', '51501790dfdabdfe6c1ec6c475ebad53', 'The station building, completed in 1905, resembles a great stone cathedral. A renovation finished in 2007 added two levels of underground platforms, turning the station into a four-level structure. Looking down from the grand staircase beneath the dome, platforms stack one below another as if the floor had dropped away, and the lowest tracks run about 18 m underground. The heavily ornamented 19th-century canopy and the bare concrete atrium share the same view, two eras joined without quite fitting together. Between trains, when the marble hall empties, only the echo of the high ceiling remains. It is a vertical transfer space, neither arrival nor departure, where people sink up and down through the levels.'),
  ('bip9TQfu', '8d27b55dfac6058ca574f095eeac614c', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Joetsu Line. The up platform is at ground level, but the down platform lies inside the Shin-Shimizu Tunnel, opened in 1967, about 70 m below the station building. From the ticket gate you descend 24 steps along a connecting passage and then a straight flight of 462 steps before finally reaching the platform. The stairway, lit by lamps at regular intervals, takes about 10 minutes to walk down, and the climb back up can take more than twice that. Only a few trains stop each day, so check the time of your return train before heading down.'),
  ('bqxwzKhM', '7c5826bffb8bdc52bbba297e6b51ddfd', 'A housing block in Marseille designed by Le Corbusier, begun in 1947 and completed in 1952. It contains 337 apartments, and seven corridors known as "interior streets" run along the length of the building. The street on the middle floors is lined with shops and a hotel, and the apartment doors are painted in red, yellow, blue and other colors. The long, windowless corridors are dim, and the view barely changes however far you walk. People still live here, and public access is limited to areas such as the shopping street and the roof. Please do not enter the residential floors without permission.'),
  ('c6QEVJB6', '1565a081dd1b0767fe832a7221534475', 'A group of water towers standing in one corner of the Tamagawa Housing complex, sometimes called a "holy land of water towers." There were once five towers, but redevelopment has reduced their number and only three remain. Rising suddenly between the residential blocks, they create a strangely unreal landscape. The moment when only the plain towers stand out against a blue sky feels like a liminal space itself. As a symbol of Showa-era housing estate culture, they have quietly gained a following among photographers.'),
  ('cEFCuYwU', '584c0ffb5245a25482703862dd3d6db0', 'A former post office building in a hamlet in the mountains of Hitachiomiya, Ibaraki. Because the sign under the eaves, reading "Hachiri Post Office," is written from right to left, it appears to date from before the war. It went out of use around 1991, when a new post office was built next door. The pink wooden walls, decorative window frames, and a vent shaped like the postal mark at the top of the facade still remain, and a statue of Ninomiya Kinjiro stands to one side. The ceiling and other parts are collapsing. The current Hachiri Post Office operates right next door, so please do not enter the grounds and only look from outside, so as not to disturb its customers.'),
  ('ccoyejXJ', 'bb1fa80a34c078fc5056f340ff8926a9', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Iida Line, set partway up a cliff in the Tenryu River gorge. A huge concrete retaining wall towers behind the platform, and beneath the tracks the ground drops into the valley. There is no station building, only a small shelter on the platform. It cannot be reached by car; from beside the tunnel, a narrow mountain path climbs for about 20 minutes to the nearest village. It opened in 1935 as a stop on the Sanshin Railway. Some local trains pass through without stopping, so check the timetable before getting off here.'),
  ('cybiKBzm', '79432b7df0861f7901f4d9ce91b05fef', 'The government district of Chandigarh, India, designed by Le Corbusier from the 1950s onward. Its three buildings, the High Court, the Legislative Assembly and the Secretariat, stand far apart across a vast concrete plaza, with reflecting pools and monuments such as the Open Hand placed between them. The distances between the buildings are so great that they never seem to get any closer as you walk. It was inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 2016. As a working government complex, it can only be visited on guided tours that require advance registration and photo ID.'),
  ('dWApnxeZ', '3e8e998f3a3d9c64ce394a727be500f9', 'A Meiji-era wooden playhouse in the town center of Asakawa, Fukushima. Built around 1902 with a revolving stage, it was used for plays, rokyoku (narrative singing) and films. It is also known as the stage where Haruo Minami made his name in his younger days as a rokyoku performer. It closed as a cinema around 1969 and was later used as a restaurant and for other purposes. Today the central roof has fallen in, and the town has surrounded the building with traffic cones. It stands in a residential neighborhood, so please only look quietly from the street.'),
  ('dh6nPPd6', '661c08f38fb1e52b35a67c8737b942af', 'An underground space left after nearly 70 years of quarrying Oya stone, from 1919 to 1986. It covers about 20,000 square meters, reaches 60 m below ground at its deepest, and stays at around 8°C all year round. Huge walls and pillars of stone, cut in straight lines, stretch on endlessly in the dim lighting, as if you had wandered into a temple forgotten deep underground.'),
  ('dibLtfvP', '061d1690c05ba125ee661e8f86e78eff', 'An art museum designed by SANAA (Kazuyo Sejima and Ryue Nishizawa), opened in 2004. A circular glass building 112.5 m in diameter sits in the middle of a lawn, with white corridors and courtyards linked like a maze. The free public zone stays open until 10 p.m., and at night, when the visitors thin out, it becomes hard to tell which side is the front and which is the back.'),
  ('eJ7VP53r', '48e474e248cf96be70570df7cbde9da3', 'One of the older public housing estates in Hong Kong, built between 1962 and 1964. The outer walls of its blocks are painted in bands of red, orange, yellow, green and blue. From the basketball court on the roof of the multi-story car park, a facade of windows shaped like the color bands rises straight ahead, and in photos the sense of depth seems to disappear. A phased redevelopment was decided in 2024, so this view will not last forever. Many people still live on the estate, so please do not get in the way of people using the court, and do not point your camera at homes or residents.'),
  ('eKpNs9Zn', '8ba274b9ba649c50aa9ae68e82045eb7', 'A former pharmacy in a Western style, standing quietly in the Katakai neighborhood of Kujukuri, Chiba. Its white-painted clapboard walls and arched entrance porch give it a faintly foreign air. The paint is peeling and the windows are boarded up, yet its state of decay, just short of collapse, has a curiously stylish charm, and in summer it hides quietly behind thick vegetation.'),
  ('eZkorbK7', '48a8d66afa0362e3134c6c664f0b3884', 'A row of commercial buildings stretching about a kilometer through central Seoul, from Jongno to Toegye-ro. Based on a concept by Kim Swoo-geun, eight buildings were built between 1967 and 1968, and seven remain today. It was once a major market for electronic components, but trade shifted to Yongsan in the late 1980s, and today shuttered units and small workshops sit side by side. Elevated walkways on the third floor connect the buildings, and as the corridors of identical width go on, you lose track of which building you are in. The Seoul city government plans to remove the walkways in stages from 2026, so the sections open to walk through may change.'),
  ('eiSnWa89', '28d9eb319d64a168d70d505c21a464c8', 'An entertainment district in Gifu City, known throughout Japan from the Showa-era popular song "Yanagase Blues." In 1960, the first all-weather arcade in the prefecture was built over Yanagase-dori. Since the department stores left, a dim street lined with shutters runs on beneath the long roof, even in the daytime. Part of the arcade on the north side is scheduled to be removed over about ten years because of its age.'),
  ('ep5tNFNY', 'd01ec368fdbc10e7a328380cc2eb624d', 'Wartime remains scattered across the farmland of Maehama in Nankoku, Kochi, south of Kochi Ryoma Airport. They are semi-cylindrical concrete hangars built during the Pacific War to protect aircraft of the Kochi Naval Air Base from air raids. There were once 41 of them, large and small; seven remain today, and they were designated a historic site of Nankoku City in 2006. Shelter No. 1 still bears bullet holes from strafing by US aircraft, and the largest, No. 4, is 44 m wide. Some are used as storehouses by farmers, so please do not enter the fields or property and view them from the road.'),
  ('es2eLiRx', '34d25706d57ed84bd2dcded89887c410', 'An underground bunker dug beneath Donzurubo in Kashiba, Nara, where white tuff rock surfaces roll on like waves. In the summer of 1945, the army dug it as a combat command post for the General Air Army, in preparation for a decisive battle on the Japanese mainland. The tunnels are divided into two systems, with a total length said to be about 2 km. Since 1967, part of it has been used by Kyoto University as an observatory for crustal movement. There is a risk of collapse and entry is prohibited, so please only look at the entrance from outside and visit the interior when a guided viewing event is held.'),
  ('etS9YLnP', 'ffab88caa40d2e24467fa8f8d8a1c483', 'A former terminal at John F. Kennedy International Airport in New York, designed by Eero Saarinen and opened in 1962. It is known for its thin concrete shell roof, like outstretched wings, and an interior made entirely of curves, with red-carpeted tube-shaped passages that once led to the boarding gates. After closing in 2001, it reopened in 2019 as the lobby of the TWA Hotel. Walking through the curved white passages feels like being sealed inside a 1960s vision of the future.'),
  ('fFLMGMbg', 'ace9ad2ce3654c1228676b2ebcb097e3', 'Bond Street station on the Elizabeth line opened in October 2022, five months after the line itself. Its tunnels, larger in cross-section than those of the older Underground, are lined almost seamlessly with curved cream-colored panels of glass-fiber reinforced concrete, and the lighting is mainly indirect, softly washing the walls. Because every station is finished with the same materials and the same light, standing in a curved passage or stairway, you can lose track of which station you are in.'),
  ('fMm9FwDX', 'e5312b5f3a21b9843d7bbe2164df4513', 'An underground passage linking the office buildings of the Marunouchi area, bright and wide, with long glass display cases running along both sides. During the day it is busy with office workers, but in the early morning or on holidays, when few people are around, it falls silent even though it lies at the heart of the Marunouchi business district, creating a strange atmosphere cut off from the noise of the real world. The orderly glass cases and the long, straight passage have drawn attention on social media as a liminal space that seems to sit on the boundary between the everyday and the extraordinary.'),
  ('ffbCQ6bM', 'e1592231bc1b5fd469c28b2569b511a9', 'A reinforced concrete shaft tower built by the former Imperial Japanese Navy as the pithead of a coal mine. It is the only prewar tower-type winding tower left in Japan, and one of only three remaining in the world. The rugged mass of concrete rises abruptly from a residential neighborhood with a strange presence. The surrounding area has been landscaped as a green park, and the contrast of a huge military and industrial ruin left standing alone amid an everyday scene gives it a peculiar sense of unreality.'),
  ('g3Pub95b', '52e845478d56552b8e594683ac570e3c', 'Kamataen, a small amusement park spread across the roof of Tokyu Plaza Kamata, a commercial building in front of Kamata Station. It has the "Ferris Wheel of Happiness," the only rooftop Ferris wheel in Tokyo, along with train-shaped rides and inflatable play equipment, carrying on the spirit of a rooftop amusement park that has operated since 1968. The contrast between the station crowds below and the Ferris wheel gondolas overhead is distinctive, giving a sense of being out of the ordinary, as if left behind in a gap between buildings.'),
  ('g5SSFruW', '1011cf35ff58b50c28fe37bba1b73061', 'The second floor of a multi-tenant building in Akasaka, completed in 1976. From September 1 to 13, 2026, a vacant glass-fronted unit here displayed a life-size "real 3D advertisement" for a limited time, marking the Japanese release of the film "Backrooms." Created by the creative team PINPIN STUDIO, it recreated a space from the film by stacking reused items, and at night a yellow glow appeared in the alley. The display could be viewed through the glass from the street, and posts by people who saw it spread on social media.

The display has ended and the space has returned to being an ordinary vacant rental office, so please refrain from visiting.'),
  ('gE3dSWYN', '788168a6e3912f449211ea8b0827b019', 'The disused track bed of the former JNR Kurayoshi Line, which became widely shared on social media as "the most beautiful abandoned railway in Japan." Forty years after the line closed in 1985, a bamboo grove has grown up along the old route, its tall green stalks lining and enclosing the track. The contrast between the tunnel of bamboo, with sunlight filtering through, and the rusted rails creates an unreal space where time seems to have stopped. Local volunteers have cleared and preserved the route, and you can still walk along it and trace what remains of the old line.'),
  ('gEpPMKQ6', '2bd7e31a888119bbbf475c9cdbeeb016', 'Egypt''s new capital, announced in 2015 and under construction in the desert east of Cairo. It is planned to cover about 700 km² and house 6.5 million people. Parliament and ministry buildings, the Iconic Tower (the tallest building in Africa), and a vast mosque and cathedral stand along wide avenues that meet in straight lines. Many ministries moved here in 2023, and in 2024 it officially began functioning as the seat of government, but few people live here yet, and many of its streets remain new and quiet.'),
  ('gHBKZTqy', '4e7b9d9a1804706bb6944afad4e92d41', 'The remains of Kawano Station on the Okutama Lake Ropeway, which opened in 1962 and stopped running in the winter of 1966 for what was called a "winter closure," never to reopen. A rusted gondola is still here, sitting quietly as if buried in a bamboo grove. It was built to carry people, yet no one will ever ride it again, and the place feels as though time has stopped. There are no-entry signs and a risk of collapse, so please do not approach it; view it only from a distance.'),
  ('gTe8qwaP', '47d1fbbff2b5cefa525af5b7099dce09', 'The remains of a hotel on Mt. Washu in Shimotsui, Kurashiki. It was built around the time the opening of the Seto Ohashi Bridge (1988) brought more tourists, and in 1990 a rotating observation tower called the "Rainbow Tower," rising to 138 meters, was added, but the facility closed in 1997. The rusted tower and the building with its broken windows remain on high ground overlooking the Seto Inland Sea. The grounds are closed off with barbed wire and no-entry signs, and in the two-plus years up to March 2022, 35 people were caught for trespassing and similar offenses. Please view it only from the nearby public road.'),
  ('gf7SinSV', 'be52cf5aa808408657b3f9942ba43f95', 'The site of Makabe Station, an intermediate stop on the Tsukuba Railway''s Tsukuba Line, which closed in 1987. The old platform remains as it was, and the site now serves as a cycling rest area on the Tsukuba Kasumigaura Rin Rin Road. The tracks have been removed, but the platform edge stones and traces of the station sign quietly recall the past, and the empty, straight stretch of space has an unusual, out-of-the-ordinary feel. It is especially dreamlike when the rows of cherry trees are in bloom.'),
  ('gu6VsXgq', 'b76fd0576bce3c53083f91bd5c7f911a', 'The remains of an elementary school in Garo, a district in the mountains of Bibai, Hokkaido, that once thrived as a town of the Mitsubishi Bibai coal mine. At its peak more than 1,800 children attended, and in 1959 and 1960 two round, three-story reinforced concrete school buildings were built. Joined by a connecting corridor, they are said to have looked like a pair of eyeglasses from above. The school closed in 1974 after the mine shut down. Today one round building and the gym remain in the forest, and the ground floor is filled with water. It lies deep in the woods rather than along the road and is not an open facility. The building is crumbling and bears appear in the area, so do not try to get close.'),
  ('iASM2FaZ', '3bc8b268d40ddd4223d74c6a8dc1bd7a', 'Part of a housing complex built as national civil servant housing for Tsukuba Science City. It fell out of use as the government cut back on staff housing, and although it is surrounded by parks, condominiums, and family neighborhoods, this one section alone remains empty. An everyday residential area sits right next to a housing complex where time has stopped. It is on national land and entry is not permitted, so please view it only from outside. The former housing sites are being sold off, so it may disappear in the future.'),
  ('ib3BSyoC', 'a0daa754d07cdb82875f2c5a986e7aac', 'A main post office designed by the architect Janko Konstantinov as part of the reconstruction plan after the 1963 earthquake. The main building and tower were completed in 1974, and the circular counter hall, whose radiating concrete ribs open like petals, in 1982. Beneath the hall''s dome was a mural depicting the earthquake and the rebuilding. A fire in 2013 destroyed the dome and the mural, and the hall still stands without a roof. The main building is used for post office offices, and restoration of the hall is at the planning stage.'),
  ('ipqatQzY', '9f45af0c91c410954a99c3e8b91014d6', 'The remains of a hydroelectric power plant in Yano, Kamikawa, Saitama, in the valley of the Kanna River. It began operating in 1914 and is said to have been the first hydroelectric plant in Saitama Prefecture. When the Shimokubo Dam was built upstream, part of its intake channel was to be submerged, and the plant was decommissioned in 1966. The machinery is long gone, but the solid brick building, still fitted with insulators, stands to this day. The site is privately owned, and the town of Kamikawa states that it is not in a position to permit entry. Please do not enter the grounds; view it only from outside.'),
  ('jdR4Gh8o', '1a4f68540556341a1b809d77c6b2a4d1', 'A 14-story building in Whittier, Alaska, where most of the town''s roughly 270 residents live. Built in the 1950s as an Army facility, it now houses not only apartments but also a post office, a general store, the police, city offices, a clinic, and a church under one roof. It is connected to the school by an underground tunnel, so in winter people can take care of daily errands without going outside. Outside the windows are the fjord and the mountains; inside, corridors of identical doors stretch on. It is a residential building, so please be considerate of the residents when visiting.'),
  ('jwojecgD', 'd1bdf0daee9c3fb6baa6cb9f90f89cbc', 'Nagoya''s oldest underground shopping street, opened in 1957. Because it was built together with the Higashiyama subway line, the original passage follows the tracks in a wide curve. Similar shopfronts continue along a curving passage where you cannot see far ahead, and in the empty hours before the shops open, it feels as though no matter how far you walk, you keep ending up back in the same place.'),
  ('kEXNdF9Y', 'ff6ddc99f9cc4ee44449adea21ad79ad', 'Twin cylindrical towers along the Chicago River designed by Bertrand Goldberg; the 65-story towers were completed in the early 1960s. The upper floors are apartments, with fan-shaped units and semicircular balconies arranged in a radial pattern, while the lower 19 floors are an open spiral parking garage with no exterior walls. Cars are parked by attendants, and the sight of them working their way up around the circle is clearly visible from outside. At night, the lit spiral floors look like an urban void made of the same view stacked again and again. The upper floors are homes, so please admire it from outside.'),
  ('kFtXvGrQ', '3c10ee5775dae04b865d1e3ba3e4b4d2', 'Kintetsu''s Uji-Yamada Station, opened in 1931 as the gateway to Ise. Designed by Setsu Kuno, its 128-meter-wide facade is clad in cream-colored terracotta tiles, and the roof is topped with Spanish tiles. It is a Registered Tangible Cultural Property of Japan and is still in use as a station. At night, after the waves of pilgrims to Ise have ebbed, the high-ceilinged concourse holds a stillness in which you lose track of what era it is.'),
  ('kLSQhNsf', '51fb148f6ad5df0f1659b56109569da2', 'One of the apartment blocks on Ikeshima where coal-mining families lived until the mine closed in 2001. A remnant of the coal-mining town that once supported most of the island''s population, its concrete walls are covered with ivy and weeds, and even the balcony railings are buried in green. The abandoned blocks lined up on high ground overlooking the sea let you imagine the bustle of the past, yet now they simply stand in quiet decay, and the whole island has the air of a museum where time has stopped.'),
  ('kk5yFE6g', '5637c632ecdaba50a45a3f39e983fd50', 'The "Sora no Eki" (Sky Station), an observation facility 41 m above the ground, built on preserved piers of the old Amarube Viaduct (Amarube Bridge), where a train fell from the bridge in an accident in 1986. When the bridge was replaced with a new concrete one in 2010, part of the old structure was deliberately left standing and reborn as a walkway and observation deck. The aerial walkway, looking down on the Sea of Japan and the village of Amarube, is an unusual high-up space that holds the memory of the tracks where trains once ran. It is also a place of remembrance that keeps the memory of the accident alive.'),
  ('kmzMLT99', '8fb5ea2341da29408c99f13640b1d548', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Dosan Line. It sits at the bottom of a valley in the Asan Mountains near the border between Tokushima and Kagawa, with no road for cars leading to it. It opened as a signal station in 1929 and became a passenger station in 1950. Because the grade is steep, it is a switchback station: trains that stop here first reverse on a spur track before entering or leaving the platform. An old wooden station building remains, and only three local trains in each direction stop here. The mountain paths are slippery, and some sections along the cliffs have no railings.'),
  ('kusiVgvC', 'dd076f932af39ff357e60ef54950deaa', 'The remains of Tateishiyama Station on the Shidaka Ropeway, which opened in 1984 and ended service after only about 15 years, following typhoon damage in 1998. It has been left forgotten in the mountains for more than 20 years since, and rust-brown gondola carriers and the crumbling stairs of the station building appear suddenly in the quiet forest. The sight of a ride that once carried tourists slowly decaying gives off a peculiar, floating feeling, as if time had stopped. The footing is poor and there is a risk of collapse, so take great care if you explore.'),
  ('m52bzS9d', 'df0062a321b6a04ab1fbe73cc58a139b', 'An aquarium that closed at the end of March 2021 and now stands abandoned. Affectionately known as "the aquarium where ocean sunfish swim," it was famous for its penguin and sunfish exhibits, but since closing only the white building remains, quietly overlooking Ago Bay. It is private property, so please stay off the grounds and view it only from outside.'),
  ('m7ojMcHo', '5361dc2770fbaeaa4471b48ef92568aa', 'A small ferry that links Wakamatsu and Tobata across Dokai Bay in Kitakyushu, a one-way trip of about three minutes. It has run since the Meiji era, and even after the Wakato Bridge was built directly overhead in 1962, service continued at the request of local residents. The sight of the little boat crossing back and forth beneath the huge red bridge, together with the old ferry landings, carries an air of having drifted slightly out of step with the times.'),
  ('mAp7XKRg', '7a769c242c6833e15d86be2979fd2ccc', 'A small town on the eastern shore of California''s Salton Sea, 68 meters below sea level. The lake formed in 1905 when river water overflowed from an irrigation canal, and in the 1950s and 60s it thrived as a lakeside resort, but rising salinity and mass fish die-offs drove people away. Today the population is just over 200. The "Drive-In" on the edge of town is an artwork by artists connected with the Bombay Beach Biennale: old cars and boats lined up in front of a white trailer that serves as the screen. People live nearby, so please visit quietly.'),
  ('mUJPSbt5', '50c7273ce87b4f7922e0d866a28b510b', 'Completed in 1955, the Asakusa Underground Street is said to be the oldest surviving underground shopping street in Japan. It was opened by local Asakusa merchants as a shopping arcade connected directly to Asakusa Station on the Ginza subway line. The ceiling is low, and small eateries steeped in the atmosphere of the Showa era line its curving passage. Fluorescent light and well-worn tiled walls create a strange sense of time, and it is surprising to find such a dense other world just beneath the tourist district of Asakusa.'),
  ('mmjcDRkP', '35b6611b8e7d4e20659a38e42d86cc0d', 'The remains of a frozen food factory along Route 119 (the Nikko Kaido) in Nikko. It is unclear when it closed, but it has gone unused for a long time, and part of the roof has badly buckled. Ivy climbs over the gate facing the road, and several buildings and old vehicles remain further back on the site. It is private property, so please view it only from outside.'),
  ('n5PKtWce', 'bb13cb12b6ba545b0c8e7e4ae7e19553', 'A brick arch bridge on the former Shin''etsu Main Line, completed in 1893. It is 91 meters long, stands 31 meters above the riverbed and was built with about two million bricks. About 6 km of the abandoned line, from Yokokawa Station to the site of Kumanodaira Station, has become the Abt Road walking trail, which lets you cross the bridge on foot and pass through a series of tunnels. Walking along a track where trains no longer run, you feel left behind halfway down a passage that has finished its work.'),
  ('nAxdFr63', '5f2812e8406ccd40b9b6a5b65685dafc', 'A housing complex designed by the architect Moshe Safdie for Expo 67 in Montreal. It stacks 354 precast concrete boxes, with outdoor walkways and stairs running between them. Other homes sit both overhead and underfoot, and the paths turn and climb upward while the line between inside and outside stays blurred. Built as an experimental housing project for the Expo, it is still owned and lived in by its residents. The grounds are private property, so see the inner walkways on a guided tour and respect the residents'' privacy.'),
  ('nHzr9PVP', 'd7d30b8f669b0a8401b1b7e32f54ee46', 'The site of the former Yubari City Asahi Elementary School, directly across from the Yubari Coal Mine Museum. It opened in 1975 when three elementary schools merged, using the building of the former Yubari No. 2 Elementary School, but closed just eight years later, in 1983. It was then used as a lodging facility called "Family School Fureai" until that closed in 2006. The facility''s sign still hangs on the school building with its broken windows, and the gymnasium roof collapsed under heavy snow in 2021. The interior is at risk of collapse, so please view it only from the roads around the museum.'),
  ('nPuRMQQp', '6fa734b1ba67e381c15543d9373d4f0d', 'An unstaffed, 24-hour vending machine corner standing quietly along a national highway. It once served late-night drivers as a "coin snack" rest stop, offering light meals, drinks and even sundries, but today many of the machines have stopped and only a few are still running. Inside, with no one around and only the fluorescent lights shining in neat rows, the room is bathed in an unreal, even brightness, and a late-night visit brings a strange, floating feeling reminiscent of the Backrooms.'),
  ('ncUNG4h3', '7435036d425294c5a5ff32ea6deb86be', 'The site of a plant that processed ore from the Sado Gold Mine, known for its huge concrete structures stepping down the mountainside like terraces. It is said to have once been the largest of its kind in the East, and today it has a striking presence as an ivy-covered ruin. When it is lit up at night it takes on a dreamlike look, but the structures are aging, so please do not enter the restricted areas.'),
  ('nxrtUmie', '10560338c309fd03832a7629f25eb08e', 'An unstaffed station on the JR Iida Line, beside the Tenryu River near the point where Shizuoka, Aichi and Nagano prefectures meet. It opened in 1936 as a station on the Sanshin Railway, but when Sakuma Dam was completed in 1956, part of the riverside settlement was submerged, and in time everyday life disappeared from the surrounding area. No road for cars leads to the station, and the nearest settlement is about an hour''s walk away along a mountain path. The wooden station building remains, along with the ruins of houses and a tea factory.'),
  ('o5t5vXLu', 'c1da2c0f140a3f74a6bc3be23218511b', 'The site of the former village of Tokuyama, now at the bottom of the Tokuyama Dam reservoir in Ibigawa, Gifu. About 1,500 people (466 households) once lived in eight mountain hamlets, but every household relocated for the dam''s construction, and the village was dissolved in 1987. Completed in 2008, Tokuyama Dam is said to be the largest in Japan, with a total storage capacity of 660 million cubic meters. The places where people once lived now lie beneath the emerald-green lake. Upstream, the Tokuyama Kaikan has exhibits on life in the former village.'),
  ('o7nfsxtg', '69d572aeeb2c3266cdd31e581d653f27', 'A 17-story apartment building designed by the architect Rista Šekerinski and completed in 1963. The zigzag of triangular terraces jutting from its facade earned it the nickname "Toblerone." When it was finished there were no other tall buildings around it, and its shape drew both ridicule and debate. Even now the tower rises abruptly from the low houses around it, preserving a Yugoslav-era vision of the future just as it was. People still live here, so please view it quietly from outside and do not point your camera at homes or residents.'),
  ('oERyKFTJ', 'dc4aed5b397b4ee1948e81f3929d20d1', 'A station on the Chollima Line of the Pyongyang Metro, opened in 1987 together with Yonggwang Station. It lies about 100 meters underground and is reached by a long escalator from the surface. An arched ceiling stretches over a platform of polished marble, and the light of chandeliers hung at regular intervals is reflected in the floor. Large murals hang on the walls, and it is one of the stations to which foreign visitors have been taken. The lavish decoration and the sheer depth together create a space cut off from the world above.'),
  ('oVquLazQ', 'f2c9eee3baf4e80106240d898887735b', 'A ropeway to the summit of Mt. Bizan in Tokushima, opened in 1957. The base station is inside the Awa Odori Kaikan building; you board from an upper floor and reach the 290-meter summit in about six minutes. The station tucked inside a building, and the summit station on a quiet evening or at night, become places where you lose track of where you are headed.'),
  ('odS8ze5z', 'b4229c10c579a167b2c1aa03ed14f2bb', 'A public housing complex in the southwestern suburbs of Rome, designed by a group of architects led by Mario Fiorentino. Construction began in 1975, and residents started moving in from 1982. A single building about 1 km long holds around 1,200 homes, earning it the nickname "Serpentone" (the big serpent). The fourth floor, intended for shops and public facilities, was never used as planned and was long occupied as housing, but in recent years it has been converted into proper apartments. Many people still live here, so please do not go inside.'),
  ('oemoYTfG', 'eb74eb50847c76eff0597f1a5e799b78', 'The remains of a brick hydroelectric power station on the upper Shikabe River in Shikabe, Hokkaido. It was the third of the power stations that the Hakodate Suiden company built around Onuma; it began operating in 1919 and was decommissioned in 1965 together with the first and second stations. The building sits behind Hokkaido Electric Power''s Shikabe substation, its roof has collapsed, and in summer it is buried in vegetation and almost hidden. It lies inside the fence of an active power facility, so please do not enter and view it only from outside.'),
  ('osSBC4sM', '2bb755d3281d6000a3e6d26fe0932c6b', 'The remains of a love hotel facing an inlet in Toba. It consists of a cottage-style older wing and a two-story newer wing, and appears to have closed in the 2010s. The cluster of buildings, slowly being overtaken by plants, seems frozen in time right next to a tourist area. It is private property, so please stay off the grounds and view it only from outside.'),
  ('otDUUTkK', '2199190943bcff4509160c8590790048', 'A large exhibition hall on the 4th floor of the World Import Mart Building at Sunshine City in Ikebukuro. Right after load-in or load-out, and in the empty hours before and after events, all that remains is a vast floor with no fixtures or visitors and uniform lighting covering the entire ceiling, giving the impression of aisles that go on forever. Combined with its closed, windowless structure, this has repeatedly led people on social media to point out its Backrooms-like quality.

That sense of deja vu made it the venue for "Q LIMINAL," an immersive event marking the Japanese release of the film The Backrooms. During its run from September 11 to 20, 2026, it drew attention as a place to experience in real life the "endless, empty exhibition halls" you would expect to find only in games and films. (The event has ended. The exhibition hall can be entered only when events are being held.)

photo: Q LIMINAL'),
  ('pW5UhxeC', '7680d3edd5ed6f4dd6f1c2535eeb2186', 'The remains of a small wooden school, reached by heading north on Hokkaido Road 550 from Horonuka in Rumoi. It began as Naka-Horonuka Elementary School, which opened in 1905; as the number of pupils fell it became a branch of Horonuka Elementary School in 1971, and it closed in 1983. When it closed, there was reportedly just one teacher and one pupil. The gymnasium has collapsed, but the schoolhouse and the teachers'' housing remain beside the road. The classroom ceilings have fallen in and the walls are crumbling, so please do not approach the buildings and view them only from the road.'),
  ('pZ2q6Ugg', '7fa8f3393c76294c9fda51cffa516610', 'The site of one of Japan''s most historic mines, said to have a mining tradition going back to the Asuka period. It was long open to the public as a historic site (from fiscal 2026 it is a tour facility by advance reservation only). Inside the tunnels, reddish-brown rock faces and the traces of mine cart tracks remain, and above ground the remains of the old ore-processing facilities stand quietly among cracked concrete.'),
  ('piDZag2m', '076875508589c4cde2d03d9d588147c9', 'An observation hall on the 32nd floor of the Gunma Prefectural Government Building, a 153.8-meter tower completed in 1999. The entire floor, 127 meters above the ground, is open to the public free of charge until 10 p.m. Looking out from the empty floor at night toward Gunma''s three famous mountains, the Jomo Sanzan, and the lights of the Kanto Plain, you forget that you are inside a government office.'),
  ('pjaXkzcy', '047f9f4f36db38a4e22dcd9d7ac031a1', 'A new district of Ordos in China''s Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region. Construction aimed at a population of around one million began around 2004, and the city government moved here in 2006. Residents were slow to arrive, and from around 2009 it was reported around the world as a "ghost town," but the population has since grown and more than 100,000 people now live here. Around the plaza stand the Ordos Museum designed by MAD, which looks like a mass of metal, a library shaped like a row of books, and a grand theater that recalls a Mongolian headdress, while only a few people cross the vast paved expanse.'),
  ('prqyoo97', 'f79a4cf35914dfa54401807ae59c636e', 'A small wooden schoolhouse in the hills of Kamogawa, Chiba. It opened in 1874 as Nishi Elementary School and in 1909 was merged with nearby schools to become a branch school of Soro Elementary School. It closed in 1967. Mikio Mizuta, the founder of Josai University, attended the school, and a monument and information board stand on the grounds. The schoolhouse floors and roof have collapsed and the site is off-limits, so please view it only from Prefectural Road 89 or from the neighboring shrine.'),
  ('qDakyLU3', 'e5e70e2e4427cc4b30f9c0efe910f0b1', 'A German colonial-era mining town near Lüderitz in southern Namibia. It was built after a railway worker found a diamond in 1908, and came to have a hospital, a theater and even an ice factory. When richer deposits were found to the south in 1928, people moved away, and by 1956 the town was deserted. Since then, sand from the Namib Desert has crept into the buildings: between pale walls the floors have turned into slopes, and the corridors are buried in sand. It lies within a restricted area, so a permit is required to visit, and you should follow the guidance of local tours.'),
  ('qLkZzgjt', 'f53d1224d64ac0e03fdaecd93c592be8', 'The remains of an early Showa-era hydroelectric power station standing in the reservoir of Nanairo Dam, with the lower half of the building underwater. It was built in 1931 on the Kitayama River in Shimokitayama, Nara, and generated power with water carried through a tunnel from upstream. Later, Nanairo Dam was built downstream, the river became a lake, and the building was left standing in the water. Now only the upper floor with its row of windows shows above the surface, and the building looks stranded in the middle of the still lake. Entry to the building has been prohibited since 2018, so please view it only from a distance.'),
  ('qRzDQ5vp', 'ce54d0ceef0f737070ca3ab591333f30', 'A new city built by China''s CITIC Construction on the outskirts of Luanda, the capital of Angola. The first phase was completed around 2011: about 750 apartment buildings of 5 to 13 stories, laid out in a grid with facades in different colors. Right after completion, prices were so high that almost nothing sold, and foreign media called it a "ghost town," but price cuts and mortgage support brought residents in from 2013 onward, and more than 100,000 people are now said to live there. The view of identical buildings repeating over and over is still unique. It is a residential area, so please be considerate of residents when taking photos.'),
  ('qT48wnpH', '38329d3490fa44370d7b9f7d9ec7234c', 'A gallery-style observation lobby on the top floor of the Tochigi Prefectural Government main building, completed in 2007. A glass-walled corridor about 65 meters above the ground wraps all the way around the building, and it is open free of charge until 9 p.m. on weekdays and weekends alike. After office hours, looking out at the night view of Utsunomiya from the empty corridors of a government building, you feel as if you have stepped slightly outside of reality.'),
  ('qgSBZ4HG', '6a74119790a31c1fb183895f80fb763a', 'The site of the Motoyama Smelter, the heart of the Ashio Copper Mine, which supported Japan''s modernization from the Meiji era through the Showa era. It was also the setting of the mine pollution incident regarded as the origin of Japan''s pollution problems, and rust-colored steel frames and decaying factory buildings still lie piled on the mountainside. The great smokestack, more than 45 meters tall, still rises from the slope as a symbol, and these industrial remains being swallowed by greenery suggest another world where time has stopped.'),
  ('qptwvrZR', '08cafbd3217e88cf79716dc49cee8501', 'A water park beside a lake on the outskirts of Hue, Vietnam, which opened in 2004 while still unfinished. It failed to draw visitors, closed and reopened several times, and was then left abandoned for years; its huge dragon-shaped building in the middle of the lake and its grass-covered slides became known worldwide as an "abandoned amusement park." The city of Hue began renovations in 2024, and since 2025 it has been open as a free park with a walking path around the lake. The dragon building still stands, and you can climb stairs up into its mouth.'),
  ('qqCxVs6h', 'f564b613e21685fdea8347cf96943c5c', 'A red-brick tunnel on the abandoned line of the Funaki Railway, which carried coal from the Taisho era into the Showa era. Surrounded by dense trees, its moss-covered brick lining speaks of the many years that have passed. A quiet, mysterious historic site that feels like an entrance to another world.'),
  ('quiNcMMg', 'a9f2deac795a8657d013584cf934a262', 'The remains of Taiyo Elementary School, standing in the mountains of Haboro, Hokkaido, on the site of the former Haboro coal mine. The school opened in 1940, and in 1962 it gained an unusual circular gymnasium 32.7 meters across. After the mine shut down, the school closed in 1971; from 1982 it served as the town''s lodging facility, Midori no Mura (''Green Village''), until that too closed in 2001. The well-known round gym collapsed under the weight of snow in March 2018, and today the three-story reinforced concrete school building and the wreckage of the gym remain. The bridge leading to the school has been blocked to vehicles, and bears live in the area, so please keep your distance and view it only from the road.'),
  ('rAiYXPVt', '924fd08ade013191b3da61ed2aff7647', 'Okunoshima is now popular with tourists as ''Rabbit Island'', but during the war it was a secret poison gas production base that was erased from maps. The museum passes on that history today, and together with the ruins of the power plant, gunpowder stores and other structures that remain on the island, it gives the place a distinctive atmosphere in which a peaceful present and a heavy past exist side by side.'),
  ('rKT2maBC', 'c44e6e18403b48eea7358871c1e75425', 'The building of the former Jonan Junior High School of Yoshii Town, standing in the hills of Akaiwa, Okayama. In 1969 the school merged with nearby Hitomi Junior High School, and the building soon stopped serving as a school. It later passed into the hands of a company, and its interior walls, ceilings and even stairs are said to have been painted white, which earned it the name ''the white abandoned school''. It appears to have fallen out of use by the 1980s, and in the second-floor classrooms, light from the windows spreads across the white walls. It is private property, so please stay off the grounds and view it only from outside.'),
  ('rTGPj4y9', '6bd5dbfe54bf48d6da82bd3e60b5dc94', 'Winters in Montreal are harsh, and starting with the opening of Place Ville Marie in 1962, underground passages were gradually linked into a network about 32 km long. The concourse of Central Station, opened in 1943, is in the Art Deco style, and beneath the large windows at each end runs a series of bas-reliefs by Charles Comfort depicting Canadian industry and everyday life. From there, passages branch out to malls, offices and metro stations, and you can keep walking without any sense of the weather or the time of day outside.'),
  ('rvEs5gKL', 'd4e7750e1e18e7ca9a11d1a8dcba822d', 'A station on Berlin''s U5 underground line, opened in 2021. Part of it lies beneath the Kupfergraben, a canal of the Spree, and it was dug using a method that freezes the surrounding ground. The platform ceiling is a deep ultramarine, scattered with 6,662 small lights like a starry sky. It was designed by Max Dudler, based on Schinkel''s stage set for the Queen of the Night in the opera The Magic Flute. The platform lies about 16 meters underground. Once a train pulls out, only footsteps remain beneath the artificial night sky.'),
  ('sDGqDpaA', '734cf01912e332aaa11b18405ae6791b', 'In 1962 the businessman Mario Bagno bought a small hilltop village in Brianza, dating back to the Middle Ages, and turned it into a town devoted to entertainment. A minaret-like tower, a pagoda, a gate modeled on a medieval castle, a hotel and a dance hall were built, but landslides caused by heavy rains in 1976 and 1977 blocked the only road, and the town went into decline. Today the tower and colonnades still stand among the trees and grass. Many of the buildings are unsafe and sit fenced off on private land, and visiting hours are limited. Please view it only from the permitted areas.'),
  ('sNq4kVMo', '204b0a12c80e2608fde0b846a5a9f54d', 'A station on the Zamoskvoretskaya Line of the Moscow Metro in Russia, opened in September 1938. It takes its name from the nearby Sokol cooperative housing settlement, known as Sokol Village. It is a shallow station about 10 meters underground, with a single row of columns down the middle that flare out at the top to support two white vaults. Between the columns, the ceiling is lined with round recesses designed to reflect the light from the lamps. With nothing but white curved surfaces repeating, the platform becomes a hall that belongs nowhere once the train has gone. Be sure to check the latest safety information before traveling.'),
  ('sTsoVeXe', '1c83eef126a1a980a41f210e868b099c', 'A dining hall on the sixth floor of the Marukan Building, a commercial building in front of Hanamaki Station. It carries on the grand dining hall of the former Marukan Department Store and keeps alive the feel of a department store restaurant. For many years it has been busy with visitors who come for its famous Marukan soft-serve ice cream. It once closed because of the building''s age, but was revived by a group of local volunteers and is still open today. The spacious room and high ceiling preserve the atmosphere of a department store dining hall from the early 1970s, when it opened, giving a strange sense that time has stopped. A leading example of the retro dining halls that survive in regional cities, it is also popular on social media and draws a steady stream of visitors from all over Japan.'),
  ('sjnJteVJ', 'cc47a0f7f694b3f91b818841219b8280', 'Opened in 1911, this tunnel runs about 426 meters beneath the Elbe in Hamburg. From domed buildings on either bank, you descend about 24 meters below the water by large elevators or stairs, then walk through one of two tiled tubes to the other side. Small ceramic reliefs are set at regular intervals along the white walls, showing fish, crabs and shellfish, and even rats and discarded rubbish. Since 2019 it has been open only to pedestrians and cyclists. At times when no one is passing through, the century-old tiled tubes run straight on toward the vanishing point.'),
  ('srv9hfS7', 'e48f2ac9ab03d46663ef1d6ad04ed7b8', 'A station on the Strand in London that was the terminus of a short branch of the Piccadilly line. It opened in 1907 as Strand station, never attracted many passengers, and closed in 1994 because replacing its old lifts was judged not worth the cost. During the Second World War it served as an air-raid shelter where many people spent the night, and items from the British Museum''s collection were moved to safety in nearby tunnels. Today its doors open only for filming and for London Transport Museum''s ''Hidden London'' tours. On platforms where no trains arrive, old tiles and silence remain.'),
  ('stuSgTd9', '6c5eb4c2eacc0ddc5c42d09934e01b68', 'A tunnel for people on foot that links Honshu and Kyushu beneath the sea. It opened in 1958, at the same time as the road tunnel. Take the elevator down about 60 meters, and a fluorescent-lit passage about 780 meters long stretches ahead so straight that its end is out of sight. Partway along, a line on the floor marks the border between Fukuoka and Yamaguchi prefectures, and at times when few people pass, the quiet of a space built purely for getting from one side to the other becomes all the more striking.'),
  ('tDPJKqS6', '92d9e2c6f47626323b05943f7998a18f', 'The remains of a small branch school in the Shimizu area of Nishinagura, Shitara, Aichi, a short way along National Route 257 from the roadside station Agri Station Nagura. It opened in 1948 as a branch of Nagura Elementary School and closed in 1964 when it was merged into the main school. The single-story wooden school building and gateposts still stand, conveying the look of the small schools where children once studied in mountain villages. It sits within the village, so please stay off the grounds and view it quietly from the road, taking care not to disturb nearby residents.'),
  ('tWaY3hCp', '99aa78bc91094cdd94d67522f377bdb1', 'A waste incineration plant designed by Yoshio Taniguchi and completed in 2004. It was designed so that the axis running from the Peace Memorial Park toward the sea passes straight through the building. Through Ecorium, a glass-walled viewing space open to anyone, you can walk all the way to the sea side while looking down on the equipment at work. A strangely open passage that feels neither quite like an art museum nor quite like a factory.'),
  ('tZU5uY9m', 'ddbaf37cec969ffc79888887d6fdb4d1', 'At the site of the Mikobata ore dressing plant, only concrete foundations remain, stacked in tiers up the mountainside one above another, creating an eerie landscape like an ancient ruin buried in jungle. The plant buildings themselves were removed long ago, and the foundations alone silently hold up the memory of the machinery that once roared here. Known as the ''Machu Picchu of the Orient'', its hushed form is a borderland landscape where industrial memory and nature melt into each other.'),
  ('tbdAv23o', '817ca41a0152bcd4ec51098d39075e46', 'The remains of a leisure facility said to have been built around 1980 on former salt fields in Kashino, Ushimado, in Setouchi, Okayama. It reportedly had rows of A-frame pensions (small guesthouses) along with facilities for golf and tennis. After it closed, the drainage of this land, which lies below sea level, stopped, and the pensions and cars were left standing in the water. With only the triangular roofs lined up above the surface, the whole village looks as if it has sunk into time. It is private property, so please stay off the site and view it only from outside.'),
  ('tbiqnkVs', '0c31cdb7813a5f7f269529c83f0d8073', 'The remains of the Ogurasawa settlement of the Chichibu Mine (also known as the Nitchitsu Mine), deep in the Chichibu mountains along the Nakatsu River. Hiraga Gennai is said to have tried to develop a mine here in the Edo period, and at its peak in the 1960s more than 2,000 people lived in these mountains at an elevation of over 800 meters. The mine closed in 2022, many of the company houses have been lost to snow damage or demolition, and the Ogurasawa Elementary and Junior High School building was demolished in 2024. The land and buildings are managed by the mining company and cannot be entered without permission, so please view them only from the public road. The road is a dead end deep in the mountains, so also watch out for falling rocks.'),
  ('trD2fyrV', '1ee23a9823b6cdfc0293b26410fe3794', 'A 134-meter observation tower standing on a hill in Higashiyama Park. It opened in 1989 to mark the centennial of the City of Nagoya. Reached on foot along paths through the woods, with an observation room that still carries the air of the early Heisei years, it offers quiet hours on weekdays and at night, when only a few figures look down on the lights of the city.'),
  ('tsMBbRNY', 'e7c52faf17c1dbe37e6ca5690706e10d', 'The remains of a white resort hotel along Prefectural Route 97 in the Madarao Highlands of Iiyama, Nagano. It opened in the mid-1980s with just over 70 guest rooms, tennis courts, a large communal bath and an open-air bath, and hosted skiers and students on training camps. It is said to have remained in business until around 2012. Today its railings and roof have been damaged by the weight of snow, and windows are broken in many places. It is private property, so please view it only from the road.'),
  ('uAvsFVzp', 'ddd748f1054fb497fd469b7a62e1f293', 'A branch of the Lopia supermarket chain on the first basement floor of CiiNA Hirosaki, a commercial building in front of Hirosaki Station. Rows of plain fluorescent lights, tiled floors and the closed-in air peculiar to basements give it the feel of a supermarket that could be anywhere and yet exists nowhere. It drew attention on social media as a ''real-life liminal space'' hidden within an everyday place for shopping.'),
  ('uN8qRiyB', 'dc07de205dc24b743f40a6657081f5bc', 'A mud-bath hot spring in the mountains of the Myoban Onsen area, drawing its mud from Konya Jigoku, which is said to have been recorded long ago in a fudoki, one of Japan''s ancient provincial chronicles. Bluish-white mud bubbling and popping as it wells up is a sight that hardly seems of this world. Steam hangs over the mountainside, and you feel as if you have stepped into another world right beside everyday life. This genuine mud bath, created by the earth''s natural geothermal energy, has also drawn attention on social media as an otherworldly, liminal space.'),
  ('uW3b7GY2', '110662b653268aba847600eab4c03a78', 'A multilevel park in the heart of Sakae that combines a park, a bus terminal and shops in one structure. It opened in 2002. A thin layer of water covers the top of its large glass roof, the Spaceship-Aqua, and patterns of light sway across the open plaza below. Standing on the empty roof or in the plaza early in the morning or late at night, you feel as if you have been left behind inside a vision of the future that people once imagined.'),
  ('vAGShZq4', '80a9e5c6fbec19750941342fe7ce68cc', 'Opened in 1978. The stations of Prague Metro Line A have walls entirely covered with anodized aluminum panels. Round concave panels and flat ones are arranged in a regular pattern, and only the color changes from station to station: gold, silver, green, red. The hollows catch the surrounding light and glow dully, turning the whole wall into a uniform texture on which the eye cannot settle. Socialist-era state design stripped away every piece of information except the station name, and as a result the platform became a place that could be any station at all. Stand before this wall just after a train has left, and for a moment every clue to where in Prague you are disappears.'),
  ('vH39Dc9M', '2335e32695f7b05764ce67f4a67accc7', 'Beijing Daxing International Airport is an international airport on the southern outskirts of Beijing, China, opened in September 2019. The passenger terminal, designed by Zaha Hadid Architects and ADP Ingenierie, is a single enormous building with a total floor area of about 700,000 square meters, among the largest single terminals in the world. Its starfish-like plan has five concourses radiating from a central hall, designed so that every gate lies within about an eight-minute walk of the center. Inside, white columns opening into a C shape flow continuously up into the ceiling, and natural light falls to the floor through the "valleys" between them. With almost no straight lines or right angles, the same curved surfaces stretch on across your field of view, blurring your sense of direction and distance. At times when passengers are few, an empty valley made only of white curves appears.'),
  ('vHoKaWez', '62951bb560600a9fd25ef3ce82b2775c', 'A railway tunnel on the Sekihoku Main Line, opened between the end of the Meiji era and the early Taisho era (1912 to 1914) after grueling construction that relied on "tako-beya" forced labor and claimed many lives. Legends of hitobashira (people buried alive as human sacrifices) have been handed down, and during repair work in 1970 human bones were actually found inside the walls. Its portal still opens quietly deep in the mountains. The sight of diesel railcars passing in and out of the snowbound entrance carries a stillness that seems left behind by time.'),
  ('vZntGVz6', '713e7d9963edd695f923b2596e32cfa8', 'A tunnel on the old road that was closed after the New Inunaki Tunnel opened and took over its role. It became famous across Japan for urban legends and as a haunted spot, and it even became the subject of a film. Its heavy stone arch and a portal covered in countless layers of graffiti give it an uneasy atmosphere as it sits sealed off, quietly, deep in the mountains.'),
  ('vbUQ9kcZ', '5e68581fe6082d1f896ced68355b6ca0', 'An underground passage at Kiyosumi-shirakawa Station on the Toei Oedo Line. It was known for the irregular arrangement of its ceiling fluorescent lights, and the creator of the game The Exit 8 has explained that the anomaly in which the lighting falls out of order was based on this station (the passage itself was reportedly modeled on a different station). The lights were removed during LED replacement work between 2025 and 2026, and now only marks remain on the ceiling. Although it is an ordinary part of a station, its enclosed, repetitive corridor has long been counted among the classic examples of liminal spaces.'),
  ('wS95uPD7', '8ebb9b7864d091806de40f230f66ce68', 'Biblioteca Vasconcelos is a public library in Mexico City, Mexico. Designed by architect Alberto Kalach and others, it opened in May 2006. Inside a huge shell of glass and steel about 250 meters long, blocks of bookshelves hang in many layers as if floating in midair. The floors are made of see-through material, so as you walk the aisles between the shelves, identical bookcases continue both above and below you, and you lose track of which floor you are on. A whale skeleton work by Gabriel Orozco hangs in the central atrium, further blurring the sense of scale. At times when few visitors are around, all that remains is a gray space where the same shelves repeat farther and farther back, feeling less like a library than the inside of an endless hangar.'),
  ('wdv4MWRW', '6cdd42b3be9acabb11d71395f8de019e', 'An open-air museum where buildings from Hokkaido, dating from the Meiji era to the early Showa era, have been relocated or reconstructed and set out side by side. It opened in 1983, and 52 buildings stand on its grounds of about 54 hectares. No one lives on the streets lined with a town office, shops, and a station building, and only the tracks of a horse-drawn railway run straight ahead. On quiet days, the whole townscape looks like a stage set cut off from its own time.'),
  ('wmniXxM3', '3bb79b767de10bbb3d298eb60b7b20c4', 'Toyone Power Station is the site of a former hydroelectric plant in Toyone Village, Kitashitara District, Aichi. It began operating in 1918, built by Furukawa Mining to supply power to the Kune Mine. It drew water from the Onyu River in the Tenryu River system, with an output of 1,070 kW at first and 3,450 kW after expansion. Its management later passed from company to company: Tenryugawa Suiryoku Denki, Toho Electric Power, Nippon Hassoden, and Chubu Electric Power. With the construction of the Sakuma Dam downstream, it was moved about 80 m higher in 1955, but construction of the Shin-Toyone Dam began in 1969, which meant its intake weir would be submerged in the reservoir, and it stopped operating on December 31, 1971. Today its remains stand half-submerged on the shore of Lake Sakuma.'),
  ('x3hoBYtV', '73ec4c217b0925b42f6f95bdc90257ef', 'A metro station in Kyiv, the capital of Ukraine, opened in 1960. Its platforms lie about 105.5 meters underground, and it was long considered the deepest metro station in the world until it was overtaken by Hongyancun Station in Chongqing, China, which opened in 2022. The depth comes from its location on high ground on the right bank of the Dnipro River; from the surface, you ride two long escalators in succession, taking about five minutes to reach the bottom. During the Russian invasion that began in 2022, it was used as a shelter. Be sure to check the latest safety information before traveling.'),
  ('xjHULppd', '8093881587d5a51dbfac8388ac673def', 'Dongdaemun Design Plaza (DDP) is a cultural complex that opened in 2014, designed by Zaha Hadid with Samoo Architects & Engineers. Its exterior is clad in more than 45,000 aluminum panels, many of which differ from one another in size and curvature. Walls and roof flow together without a break, making it hard to tell where the outline of the building ends. The roof is a walkable park, and inside, gently rising ramps continue on and on. Late at night, the curved surfaces, their lights turned off, dully reflect only the glow of the streetlights, and the noise of the surrounding markets fades into the distance.'),
  ('yHAia3tA', '9bbdddc2efc88b9c9481cc43d2057dc7', 'The remains of a large ryokan on the riverside in Kinugawa Onsen. A long-established inn known for its large bath "Kappa Buro," which began as a bath built on the riverbed, it kept operating while adding annexes to the main building, but it went bankrupt in 1999 with debts of about 3 billion yen and became the first hotel in Kinugawa Onsen to close. Its entrance and other openings were sealed in 2022, and in 2026 Nikko City and others set up a council aimed at demolishing the abandoned hotels of the hot spring town. Floors may give way, so please view it only from the opposite bank of the Kinugawa River.'),
  ('yU2GmJuC', 'c4f8e821739373f6dc11c3afe4b98c36', 'A 234-meter seaside tower standing on reclaimed land at Momochihama. It was built as the monument of the 1989 Asia-Pacific Exposition (Yokatopia), and its exterior is covered with about 8,000 half-mirror panels. At night, the room is reflected in the windows of the observation deck 123 meters above the ground, blurring the boundary between the sea, the city, and your own reflection.'),
  ('yZFZrRtZ', 'ae588e1c9c3add46e45c580f3ba1c913', 'A high-speed railway station in Reggio Emilia, northern Italy, opened in 2013 and designed by Santiago Calatrava. A unit built from 13 different types of white steel portal frames is repeated 25 times to form an undulating roof 483 meters long. The station sits in the suburbs about 4 km north of the city center, surrounded by fields and roads. Between trains, only a wide space remains beneath the white ribs, giving the feeling of a city gateway with no city beyond it.'),
  ('ybWwuMCE', 'b3087203885ff760aa04332a108cb403', 'The remains of a power generation facility built on Okunoshima by the former Imperial Japanese Army. It was left unused after the war, and only its concrete building remains. Although it now sits within a tourist area on the island known as Rabbit Island, the ivy-covered, decaying ruin has a strange presence, making it an unusual place where the peaceful island scenery and the memory of war exist side by side.'),
  ('z2qWaUEB', '58836f4724212f4624ba4e58baef4fc4', 'A public housing estate in Tai Hang on Hong Kong Island, completed between 1975 and 1976. Two of its three blocks are cylindrical, said to be the only such form among Hong Kong''s public rental housing. Circular corridors run around a central courtyard on every floor, and when you look straight up, the same railings and doors are stacked in rings all the way to the sky. It is a space where the sense of up and down fades and only inside and outside remain. Many people still live here, and the entrances have code-locked gates; non-residents are refused entry and photography. Do not enter without permission, and please be considerate of the residents'' daily lives.'),
  ('zEybC8RS', '74b5064dadc85727fad4118427d2d52a', 'A former animal research facility in Lichterfelde, southwestern Berlin, known by the nickname Mäusebunker, meaning "mouse bunker." Designed by Gerd Hänska and Magdalena Hänska among others, it was begun in 1971 and, after an interruption, completed in 1981. It is a long, trapezoidal mass of exposed concrete, with blue ventilation pipes jutting out from its sloping walls. It fell out of use around 2019, and although demolition was considered, it was decided in 2023 to preserve it as a listed monument. The grounds are closed, so please view it from outside.'),
  ('zTqTM2wW', 'df2807eb78e0256ec2d982fd662db068', 'The remains of a kokumin shukusha (a public lodge) in Muroto, Kochi, near the Muroto Skyline road that runs over Cape Muroto. It opened in the early 1970s, around the time the Skyline opened, and closed at the end of October 2005. It had a circular wing and an unusual layout in which guests entered the third-floor lobby from a rooftop parking lot. After it closed, Muroto City looked for a buyer, but none was found, and the building remains surrounded by trees. Please do not enter the grounds, and view it only from outside.'),
  ('zodpUKpy', 'd52145003bd219224f6a13f9ff2f2e79', 'A prefectural government building designed by Kenzo Tange and completed in 1958. In 2022 it became the first postwar government office building to be designated a national Important Cultural Property. Murals by Genichiro Inokuma remain in the pilotis lined with exposed concrete columns and in the first-floor lobby, and the building is still in use as the prefectural office. In the lobby at quiet times, the air of the "open government office" envisioned after the war lingers quietly.'),
  ('zphbsPDi', '0055e32e0a4a70ef59a7aed059678e0a', 'A network of underground tunnels about 10 km in total length, dug in a rush from 1944, near the end of the Pacific War, to relocate the Imperial General Headquarters and government agencies in preparation for a final battle on the Japanese mainland. Its history includes the mobilization of many Korean laborers who were forced into harsh work. Only a small section is open to the public; wearing a helmet, you walk through dim tunnels of bare rock and are surrounded by the strange silence of a vast underground space that was left unfinished when the war ended.'),
  ('zsDBzZX3', '068b24ca9367256e04feeefaf9162e55', 'A tower shaped like an air traffic control tower, standing at Lancaster services on the M6 motorway. When it opened in 1965, its top, about 20 m high, held a waiter-service restaurant and an observation deck with views across Morecambe Bay and the surrounding hills. It closed to the public in 1989 because adequate emergency escape routes could not be provided, and was used for a while as offices and storage, but it now stands empty. It was listed at Grade II in 2012. The service area below is still open, but the top of the tower is not accessible.')
) as v(slug, description_md5, description_en)
where s.slug = v.slug
  and md5(s.description) = v.description_md5
  and s.description_en is null;

commit;
